{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,240p' cores/bench/LESSONS.md && rg -n '\\\"category\\\"\\\\s*:\\\\s*\\\"micro_opt\\\"|category: micro_opt|hyp-20260711-001-' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | tail -n 80\"","aggregated_output":"- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-002-r1s1 (improvement, +55.97%): Replacing the combinational DIV/REM cone with a shared radix-2 iterative M-unit greatly improves Fmax and routing efficiency when CoreMark's low dynamic divide frequency amortizes the resulting pipeline freezes.\n- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-001-r2s0 (broken, n/a): Opcode-qualified load-use interlocks must also qualify or zero RVFI unused-source metadata, or raw rs1/rs2 fields can expose stale pre-load values that reg_ch0 rejects even when the instruction does not architecturally consume those operands.\n- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-003-r2s2 (regression, -22.31%): Exact JAL prediction loses overall when J-immediate reconstruction and a 32-bit target adder sit directly on the fetch-to-PC critical path, so direct-target prediction must be staged or kept off the Fmax-limiting next-PC cone.\n- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-002-r3s1 (regression, -37.74%): A zero-latency two-entry fetch skid buffer can lose badly when its queue-head/live-response mux and count/valid control remain on the combinational IF-to-ID path, so limited prefetch during backend holds must amortize the front-end timing and routing cost.\n- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-003-r3s2 (regression, -44.80%): Caching resolved targets does not make prediction timing-free: an asynchronous direct-mapped micro-BTB's tag compare, counter/unconditional decision, and 32-bit target mux on the fetch next-PC cone can overwhelm CoreMark's saved control bubbles, so BTB lookup must be staged or mapped to a timing-friendly memory structure.\n- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-001-r4s0 (regression, -19.79%): Replacing a reset register array with per-entry validity bits can hurt an asynchronous 2R1W register file because indexed valid checks add muxing to both combinational read paths without making its write-first multi-read structure memory-inference-friendly.\n- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-002-r4s1 (regression, -22.95%): Sharing RV32M variants through an opcode-selected signed 33x33 product can hurt FPGA timing because widening the native 32-bit multiply and placing sign/zero-extension muxes before it outweighs eliminating parallel products; prefer native-width multiplication with post-product sign correction.\n- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-003-r4s2 (regression, -48.43%): Registering a micro-BTB lookup is insufficient when the four-entry tag/target/counter flop arrays and widened IF/ID prediction payload add enough placement and routing pressure to outweigh saving one CoreMark taken-control bubble; a staged predictor must also use compact, memory-friendly state and metadata.\n- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-003-r5s2 (regression, -59.29%): An adder-free RAS is not a timing-free return predictor when canonical-JALR decode and a 32-bit registered-stack-top mux still drive the fetch next-PC cone, so CoreMark return bubbles cannot amortize the added stack state and cross-pipeline control routing.\n- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-002-r6s1 (regression, -45.75%): Removing a load-to-store-data bubble can lose badly when the late WB-to-MEM bypass adds a register compare and 32-bit mux directly to store-data/RVFI routing, so even non-ALU forwarding must be kept off timing-sensitive cross-stage paths.\n- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-002-r7s1 (regression, -18.75%): Preselecting ALU-versus-load writeback before MEM/WB can outweigh narrower pipeline records by moving a 32-bit mux onto the load-extension-to-register setup path, so dead-payload pruning and mux relocation should be evaluated separately at high Fmax.\n- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-003-r7s2 (regression, -28.72%): Compressing a one-entry loop predictor to a 10-bit page-local tag and 12-bit target still leaves a compare and target mux on the fetch next-PC cone plus live-entry validation in EX, so CoreMark's saved loop bubbles cannot amortize even narrow combinational prediction unless lookup is staged or removed from timing-critical control paths.\n- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-002-r8s1 (regression, -17.54%): Collapsing MEM/WB into EX/MEM removes flops but also removes the timing boundary before load formatting, writeback selection, regfile commit, and RVFI fanout, so retirement-stage state can be cheaper than exposing the full dmem-to-commit cone in one cycle.\n- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-002-r9s1 (regression, -47.07%): Registering the DSP-backed MUL family loses badly when each CoreMark multiply asserts a pipeline-wide execute interlock, so a multiply stage is worthwhile only if its post-route Fmax gain exceeds the measured cost of those added hold cycles.\n- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-003-r9s2 (regression, -69.90%): A fetch-side fixed-loop predictor must give EX redirects priority and select redirect_target in the next-PC mux; otherwise misprediction recovery merely reloads the speculative or sequential PC and cannot restore architectural control flow.\n- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-001-r10s0 (regression, -45.03%): Making only cold MULH/MULHU/MULHSU iterative still loses when the new shift-add state, completion/result muxing, and pipeline-wide execute interlock add always-present routing cost, so rare dynamic use does not make a separate multi-cycle unit timing-free.\n- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-002-r10s1 (regression, -11.19%): Splitting ctrl_t into stage-specific EX/MEM and MEM/WB records can regress even with writeback timing unchanged because synthesis already prunes dead bundle bits while explicit control repacking and trap qualification add backend logic and disrupt packing.\n- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-003-r10s2 (regression, -8.54%): Reusing the fetch PC incrementer for a fixed backedge is not timing-free because selecting a 32-bit addend turns the constant PC+4 path into a muxed arithmetic cone, while the prediction marker and exact-signature recovery add cross-pipeline control cost that can outweigh one hot loop's saved bubbles.\n1:{\"id\": \"baseline-bench-2a03fe4\", \"title\": \"Baseline retest for cores/bench\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"outcome\": \"improvement\", \"fitness\": 282.82, \"delta_pct\": 0.0, \"vs_baseline\": 0.0, \"fmax_mhz\": 127.03, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4491485, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 9563, \"ff\": 1866, \"seeds\": [128.72, 127.03, 123.02], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-11T20:33:25.161626Z\", \"round_id\": 0, \"slot\": 0}\n2:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-001-r1s0\", \"title\": \"Share one multiplier across RV32M multiply variants\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The combinational ALU currently instantiates three independent 64-bit products for\\nsigned-signed, unsigned-unsigned, and signed-unsigned multiplication. Only one ALU\\noperation can execute per cycle, so the duplicated multiplier hardware adds DSP/LUT\\nusage and routing congestion without increasing throughput, likely depressing FPGA\\nFmax in a core already using 9563 LUT4s.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The combinational ALU currently instantiates three independent 64-bit products for\\nsigned-signed, unsigned-unsigned, and signed-unsigned multiplication. Only one ALU\\noperation can execute per cycle, so the duplicated multiplier hardware adds DSP/LUT\\nusage and routing congestion without increasing throughput, likely depressing FPGA\\nFmax in a core already using 9563 LUT4s.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Compute one 32x32 unsigned 64-bit product and derive MULH and MULHSU high halves with\\ntwo's-complement sign-correction subtractors. MUL continues to select the shared\\nproduct's low half and MULHU its high half. Sharing the expensive product should\\nimprove placement and timing while preserving the existing single-cycle EX latency,\\ninstruction-per-cycle behavior, ALTOPS formal branch, and all RV32M edge semantics.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the three parallel multiply expressions with one unsigned 32x32 product and sign-corrected high-half results for MULH and MULHSU.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_alu.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed and randomized cross-sign multiply vectors that verify all four MUL-family results after multiplier sharing.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the three parallel multiply expressions with one unsigned 32x32 product and sign-corrected high-half results for MULH and MULHSU.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_alu.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed and randomized cross-sign multiply vectors that verify all four MUL-family results after multiplier sharing.\"}], \"outcome\": \"broken\", \"formal_passed\": false, \"cosim_passed\": false, \"error\": \"implementation_compile_failed: \", \"slot\": 0, \"round_id\": 1}\n5:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-001-r2s0\", \"title\": \"Qualify load-use hazards by source operands\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The load-use detector compares a load destination against both raw rs1 and rs2\\nbit fields of every following instruction. Many RV32I formats do not consume one\\nor either of those fields, so immediate payload bits that happen to equal the load\\ndestination create unnecessary one-cycle bubbles in CoreMark's load-heavy code.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The load-use detector compares a load destination against both raw rs1 and rs2\\nbit fields of every following instruction. Many RV32I formats do not consume one\\nor either of those fields, so immediate payload bits that happen to equal the load\\ndestination create unnecessary one-cycle bubbles in CoreMark's load-heavy code.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Decode whether the IF/ID instruction actually reads rs1 and rs2, and qualify each\\nload-use comparison with the corresponding source-use bit. Real load dependencies\\nwill retain their required bubble, while false matches in OP-IMM, LOAD, JALR,\\nLUI/AUIPC, JAL, FENCE, and SYSTEM instructions will flow without stalling. This\\nsmall opcode-only guard should improve iterations per cycle with negligible area\\nand timing cost, while leaving forwarding, bus backpressure, retirement, and all\\narchitectural behavior unchanged.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Derive RV32IM rs1/rs2 source-use qualifiers from the IF/ID opcode and gate load-use matches so only true dependencies stall.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/zcore.sv\", \"description\": \"Pass the complete IF/ID instruction into the hazard unit while preserving the existing nret=1 top-level interface and stall priorities.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed checks that true load consumers still bubble and false matches in unused instruction fields do not stall or alter RVFI retirement.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Derive RV32IM rs1/rs2 source-use qualifiers from the IF/ID opcode and gate load-use matches so only true dependencies stall.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/zcore.sv\", \"description\": \"Pass the complete IF/ID instruction into the hazard unit while preserving the existing nret=1 top-level interface and stall priorities.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed checks that true load consumers still bubble and false matches in unused instruction fields do not stall or alter RVFI retirement.\"}], \"outcome\": \"broken\", \"formal_passed\": false, \"cosim_passed\": false, \"error\": \"formal_failed: reg_ch0\\nengine_0: finished (returncode=1)\\nSBY 17:52:06 [reg_ch0] engine_0: Status returned by engine: FAIL\\nSBY 17:52:06 [reg_ch0] summary: Elapsed clock time [H:MM:SS (secs)]: 0:00:20 (20)\\nSBY 17:52:06 [reg_ch0] summary: Elapsed process time [H:MM:SS (secs)]: 0:00:09 (9)\\nSBY 17:52:06 [reg_ch0] summary: engine_0 (smtbmc bitwuzla) returned FAIL\\nSBY 17:52:06 [reg_ch0] summary: counterexample trace: reg_ch0/engine_0/trace.vcd\\nSBY 17:52:06 [reg_ch0] summary:   failed assertion rvfi_testbench._witness_.check_flatten_checker_inst__assert_rvfi_reg_check_sv_42_56 at rvfi_reg_check.sv:42.6-42.59 step 20\\nSBY 17:52:06 [reg_ch0] summary:   failed assertion rvfi_testbench._witness_.check_flatten_checker_inst__assert_rvfi_reg_check_sv_44_60 at rvfi_reg_check.sv:44.6-44.59 step 20\\nSBY 17:52:06 [reg_ch0] DONE (FAIL, rc=0)\\n\\nFormal: 52 passed, 1 failed\\nFailed: reg_ch0\\n\\n--- reg_ch0/logfile.txt (last 30 lines) ---\\nSBY 17:51:47 [reg_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:00:00  Skipping step 11..\\nSBY 17:51:47 [reg_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:00:00  Skipping step 12..\\nSBY 17:51:47 [reg_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:00:00  Skipping step 13..\\nSBY 17:51:47 [reg_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:00:00  Skipping step 14..\\nSBY 17:51:47 [reg_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:00:00  Skipping step 15..\\nSBY 17:51:47 [reg_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:00:00  Skipping step 16..\\nSBY 17:51:47 [reg_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:00:00  Skipping step 17..\\nSBY 17:51:47 [reg_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:00:00  Skipping step 18..\\nSBY 17:51:47 [reg_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:00:00  Skipping step 19..\\nSBY 17:51:47 [reg_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:00:00  Checking assumptions in step 20..\\nSBY 17:51:48 [reg_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:00:01  Checking assertions in step 20..\\nSBY 17:52:05 [reg_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:00:19  BMC failed!\\nSBY 17:52:05 [reg_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:00:19  Value for anyconst rvfi_testbench.insn_order (rvfi_reg_check.sv:19.24-19.34): 16\\nSBY 17:52:05 [reg_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:00:19  Value for anyconst rvfi_testbench.register_index (rvfi_reg_check.sv:20.23-20.37): 27\\nSBY 17:52:05 [reg_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:00:19  Assert failed in rvfi_testbench: rvfi_reg_check.sv:42.6-42.59 (_witness_.check_flatten_checker_inst__assert_rvfi_reg_check_sv_42_56)\\nSBY 17:52:05 [reg_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:00:19  Assert failed in rvfi_testbench: rvfi_reg_check.sv:44.6-44.59 (_witness_.check_flatten_checker_inst__assert_rvfi_reg_check_sv_44_60)\\nSBY 17:52:05 [reg_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:00:19  Writing trace to VCD file: engine_0/trace.vcd\\nSBY 17:52:06 [reg_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:00:19  Writing trace to Verilog testbench: engine_0/trace_tb.v\\nSBY 17:52:06 [reg_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:00:19  Writing trace to constraints file: engine_0/trace.smtc\\nSBY 17:52:06 [reg_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:00:19  Writing trace to Yosys witness file: engine_0/trace.yw\\nSBY 17:52:06 [reg_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:00:19  Status: failed\\nSBY 17:52:06 [reg_ch0] engine_0: finished (returncode=1)\\nSBY 17:52:06 [reg_ch0] engine_0: Status returned by engine: FAIL\\nSBY 17:52:06 [reg_ch0] summary: Elapsed clock time [H:MM:SS (secs)]: 0:00:20 (20)\\nSBY 17:52:06 [reg_ch0] summary: Elapsed process time [H:MM:SS (secs)]: 0:00:09 (9)\\nSBY 17:52:06 [reg_ch0] summary: engine_0 (smtbmc bitwuzla) returned FAIL\\nSBY 17:52:06 [reg_ch0] summary: counterexample trace: reg_ch0/engine_0/trace.vcd\\nSBY 17:52:06 [reg_ch0] summary:   failed assertion rvfi_testbench._witness_.check_flatten_checker_inst__assert_rvfi_reg_check_sv_42_56 at rvfi_reg_check.sv:42.6-42.59 step 20\\nSBY 17:52:06 [reg_ch0] summary:   failed assertion rvfi_testbench._witness_.check_flatten_checker_inst__assert_rvfi_reg_check_sv_44_60 at rvfi_reg_check.sv:44.6-44.59 step 20\\nSBY 17:52:06 [reg_ch0] DONE (FAIL, rc=0)\\n\\nFull run log: /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_6-sol-rep2/cores/bench/worktrees/hyp-20260711-001-r2s0/formal/last_run-89305.log\\nPer-check logs in: /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_6-sol-rep2/cores/bench/worktrees/hyp-20260711-001-r2s0/formal/riscv-formal/cores/bench-89305/checks/<check>/logfile.txt\", \"slot\": 0, \"round_id\": 2, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-001-r2s0 (broken, n/a): Opcode-qualified load-use interlocks must also qualify or zero RVFI unused-source metadata, or raw rs1/rs2 fields can expose stale pre-load values that reg_ch0 rejects even when the instruction does not architecturally consume those operands.\"}\n8:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-001-r3s0\", \"title\": \"Eliminate false load-use stalls with canonical source metadata\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The load-use interlock compares a pending load destination with both raw source\\nbit fields of the following instruction, even when its opcode does not consume\\none or both fields. CoreMark's immediate, load, upper-immediate, jump, fence,\\nand system instructions can therefore incur false one-cycle bubbles whenever\\nimmediate bits alias the load destination. The earlier attempt established that\\nremoving those bubbles also requires canonical RVFI metadata for unused sources.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The load-use interlock compares a pending load destination with both raw source\\nbit fields of the following instruction, even when its opcode does not consume\\none or both fields. CoreMark's immediate, load, upper-immediate, jump, fence,\\nand system instructions can therefore incur false one-cycle bubbles whenever\\nimmediate bits alias the load destination. The earlier attempt established that\\nremoving those bubbles also requires canonical RVFI metadata for unused sources.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Decode rs1-used and rs2-used qualifiers once in ID, drive unused regfile read\\naddresses to x0, and carry zero source addresses and values into ID/EX for RVFI.\\nFeed those same canonical addresses to the load-use detector, so it stalls only\\non an architecturally consumed register. This should recover cycles without\\nadding a new datapath cone, while the zeroed metadata preserves formal register\\ntracking, forwarding behavior, bus-stall priority, and retirement semantics.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"high\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/decoder.sv\", \"description\": \"Emit validated rs1-used and rs2-used qualifiers for every legal RV32IM instruction class, defaulting both low for illegal and source-free encodings.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Canonicalize unused source addresses and values to zero for regfile reads, ID/EX forwarding metadata, and downstream RVFI reporting.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Use the canonical IF/ID source addresses so the load-use interlock bubbles only for true architectural dependencies.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/zcore.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire ID's canonical combinational source addresses into hazard detection while preserving the nret=1 interface and existing stall priorities.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_decoder.py\", \"description\": \"Check source-use qualifiers across R, I, load/store, branch, jump, upper-immediate, fence, EBREAK, and illegal encodings.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Verify true load consumers still stall, false raw-field aliases do not, and unused RVFI source addresses and data retire as zero under bus stalls.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"high\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/decoder.sv\", \"description\": \"Emit validated rs1-used and rs2-used qualifiers for every legal RV32IM instruction class, defaulting both low for illegal and source-free encodings.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Canonicalize unused source addresses and values to zero for regfile reads, ID/EX forwarding metadata, and downstream RVFI reporting.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Use the canonical IF/ID source addresses so the load-use interlock bubbles only for true architectural dependencies.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/zcore.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire ID's canonical combinational source addresses into hazard detection while preserving the nret=1 interface and existing stall priorities.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_decoder.py\", \"description\": \"Check source-use qualifiers across R, I, load/store, branch, jump, upper-immediate, fence, EBREAK, and illegal encodings.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Verify true load consumers still stall, false raw-field aliases do not, and unused RVFI source addresses and data retire as zero under bus stalls.\"}], \"outcome\": \"broken\", \"formal_passed\": false, \"cosim_passed\": false, \"error\": \"cosim_failed: selftest.elf\", \"slot\": 0, \"round_id\": 3}\n11:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-001-r4s0\", \"title\": \"Replace bulk register-file reset with validity bits\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The register file synchronously resets all 1,024 data bits, creating a broad\\nreset network and preventing the asynchronous-read array from being inferred\\nas an FPGA memory structure. After the iterative divider removed the dominant\\narithmetic cone, this reset-heavy array is a promising source of placement,\\nrouting, and clock-frequency overhead that provides no benchmark throughput.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The register file synchronously resets all 1,024 data bits, creating a broad\\nreset network and preventing the asynchronous-read array from being inferred\\nas an FPGA memory structure. After the iterative divider removed the dominant\\narithmetic cone, this reset-heavy array is a promising source of placement,\\nrouting, and clock-frequency overhead that provides no benchmark throughput.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Stop resetting the 32x32 data array and instead reset a 32-bit per-register\\nvalidity mask. Reads of an unwritten register return zero; each accepted write\\nstores the data and sets its validity bit, while x0 remains hardwired to zero.\\nThis exactly preserves the core's current deterministic zero-after-reset and\\nwrite-first semantics, but removes reset from 1,024 storage bits and may allow\\nYosys/Gowin to infer compact distributed memory. The reduced reset fanout and\\nrouting pressure should raise Fmax without changing IPC, pipeline timing, RVFI\\ndata, or any architectural invariant.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/reg_file.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a resettable validity bitmap, leave the data array unreset, return zero from invalid entries, and preserve same-cycle write-first bypass plus x0 behavior.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_reg_file.py\", \"description\": \"Verify zero reads after reset, independent validity of registers, write-first behavior, x0 immutability, and reset invalidation after prior writes.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/reg_file.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a resettable validity bitmap, leave the data array unreset, return zero from invalid entries, and preserve same-cycle write-first bypass plus x0 behavior.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_reg_file.py\", \"description\": \"Verify zero reads after reset, independent validity of registers, write-first behavior, x0 immutability, and reset invalidation after prior writes.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 353.82, \"delta_pct\": -19.79, \"vs_baseline\": 25.1, \"fmax_mhz\": 158.93, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4491817, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 3016, \"ff\": 1294, \"seeds\": [149.66, 165.15, 158.93], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-11T21:17:44.457162Z\", \"slot\": 0, \"round_id\": 4, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-001-r4s0 (regression, -19.79%): Replacing a reset register array with per-entry validity bits can hurt an asynchronous 2R1W register file because indexed valid checks add muxing to both combinational read paths without making its write-first multi-read structure memory-inference-friendly.\"}\n14:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-001-r5s0\", \"title\": \"Post-correct one native-width multiplier\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The ALU continuously forms three separate 64-bit products for signed-signed,\\nunsigned-unsigned, and signed-unsigned multiplication even though a\\nsingle-issue core selects only one result. The previous shared-multiplier\\nimplementation regressed because it widened the operands to 33 bits and put\\nsignedness-selection muxes before the multiplier; the earlier native-width\\npost-correction proposal never reached evaluation because its implementation\\nfailed to compile.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The ALU continuously forms three separate 64-bit products for signed-signed,\\nunsigned-unsigned, and signed-unsigned multiplication even though a\\nsingle-issue core selects only one result. The previous shared-multiplier\\nimplementation regressed because it widened the operands to 33 bits and put\\nsignedness-selection muxes before the multiplier; the earlier native-width\\npost-correction proposal never reached evaluation because its implementation\\nfailed to compile.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Compute exactly one native 32x32 unsigned product with no opcode or sign mux\\non either multiplier input. Derive MULH by subtracting b from the unsigned\\nhigh half when a is negative and subtracting a when b is negative; derive\\nMULHSU by applying only the a-negative correction. MUL and MULHU directly\\nselect the low and high halves. This modulo-2^32 high-half identity preserves\\nevery RV32M result while removing two multiplier cones and avoiding the\\nfailed 33x33 datapath's width and pre-multiply mux penalty. Lower DSP/LUT\\nduplication and wide-product routing should improve placement and Fmax with\\nunchanged instruction latency, IPC, divider behavior, and ALTOPS semantics.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the three parallel products with one unconditional unsigned 32x32 product and fixed-width post-product high-half sign-correction subtractors, leaving ALTOPS unchanged.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_alu.py\", \"description\": \"Add boundary and randomized cross-sign vectors for MUL, MULH, MULHU, and MULHSU to verify the post-correction identities exactly.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the three parallel products with one unconditional unsigned 32x32 product and fixed-width post-product high-half sign-correction subtractors, leaving ALTOPS unchanged.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_alu.py\", \"description\": \"Add boundary and randomized cross-sign vectors for MUL, MULH, MULHU, and MULHSU to verify the post-correction identities exactly.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 423.82, \"delta_pct\": -3.92, \"vs_baseline\": 49.86, \"fmax_mhz\": 190.37, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4491817, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 5555, \"ff\": 2086, \"seeds\": [173.85, 198.61, 190.37], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-11T21:34:10.151584Z\", \"slot\": 0, \"round_id\": 5}\n17:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-001-r6s0\", \"title\": \"Replace load barrel shift with lane muxes\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"Every load currently sends the full 32-bit dmem response through a variable\\nright shifter before byte or halfword extension. On the FPGA this general\\nbarrel-shift cone can add LUT depth and routing to the dmem-to-MEM/WB path,\\neven though RV32 loads need only four byte choices or two aligned halfword\\nchoices. CoreMark exercises this path frequently, so a small Fmax gain should\\ntranslate directly into fitness without changing its cycle count.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"Every load currently sends the full 32-bit dmem response through a variable\\nright shifter before byte or halfword extension. On the FPGA this general\\nbarrel-shift cone can add LUT depth and routing to the dmem-to-MEM/WB path,\\neven though RV32 loads need only four byte choices or two aligned halfword\\nchoices. CoreMark exercises this path frequently, so a small Fmax gain should\\ntranslate directly into fitness without changing its cycle count.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Select byte loads with an explicit four-way 8-bit lane mux and select\\nhalfword loads with a two-way 16-bit mux using address bit 1 after the\\nexisting alignment check. Feed those selected lanes into the unchanged\\nsign/zero-extension logic and bypass lane selection entirely for word loads.\\nThis should synthesize to narrower, shallower muxes than a 32-bit variable\\nshifter, reducing LUT and routing pressure while preserving load latency,\\nbus-stall behavior, misalignment traps, and all RVFI memory fields.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the variable 32-bit load-data shift with explicit byte-lane and aligned halfword-lane muxes while retaining the existing extension, trap, and RVFI logic.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed LB, LBU, LH, LHU, and LW checks across legal byte lanes plus misaligned halfword cases, including dmem backpressure and RVFI data verification.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the variable 32-bit load-data shift with explicit byte-lane and aligned halfword-lane muxes while retaining the existing extension, trap, and RVFI logic.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed LB, LBU, LH, LHU, and LW checks across legal byte lanes plus misaligned halfword cases, including dmem backpressure and RVFI data verification.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 379.85, \"delta_pct\": -13.89, \"vs_baseline\": 34.31, \"fmax_mhz\": 170.62, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4491817, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 5411, \"ff\": 2084, \"seeds\": [181.29, 159.26, 170.62], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-11T21:57:21.127340Z\", \"slot\": 0, \"round_id\": 6}\n20:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-001-r7s0\", \"title\": \"Replace register-file reset with FPGA initialization\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The 32x32 register-file data array currently places all 1,024 storage bits on\\nthe synchronous reset network. The previous validity-bitmap experiment showed\\nthat removing this bulk reset can greatly reduce storage cost, but its indexed\\nvalid checks added muxing to both asynchronous read paths and reduced Fmax.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The 32x32 register-file data array currently places all 1,024 storage bits on\\nthe synchronous reset network. The previous validity-bitmap experiment showed\\nthat removing this bulk reset can greatly reduce storage cost, but its indexed\\nvalid checks added muxing to both asynchronous read paths and reduced Fmax.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Give the register array a synthesis-visible zero power-up value and remove only\\nits synchronous reset clearing loop, while retaining the existing direct reads,\\nwrite-first bypass, and x0 handling. RV32I does not require integer registers to\\nbe cleared by a later reset, and the FPGA bitstream initialization preserves a\\ndeterministic cold start. This avoids both the 1,024-bit reset tree and the\\nfailed experiment's validity muxes, so it should reduce routing pressure and\\nimprove Fmax without changing CoreMark IPC or any retirement datapath.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/reg_file.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the bulk synchronous data-array reset loop with synthesis-supported zero power-up initialization, preserving direct asynchronous reads, write-first bypass, normal writes, and hardwired x0.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_reg_file.py\", \"description\": \"Verify cold-start zero initialization, x0 immutability, ordinary writes, and same-cycle write-first behavior without requiring a later reset pulse to erase architectural registers.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/reg_file.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the bulk synchronous data-array reset loop with synthesis-supported zero power-up initialization, preserving direct asynchronous reads, write-first bypass, normal writes, and hardwired x0.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_reg_file.py\", \"description\": \"Verify cold-start zero initialization, x0 immutability, ordinary writes, and same-cycle write-first behavior without requiring a later reset pulse to erase architectural registers.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 414.95, \"delta_pct\": -5.93, \"vs_baseline\": 46.72, \"fmax_mhz\": 186.39, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4491817, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 2905, \"ff\": 1262, \"seeds\": [156.57, 186.39, 195.5], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-11T22:12:44.983908Z\", \"slot\": 0, \"round_id\": 7}\n23:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-001-r8s0\", \"title\": \"Prune pipeline payload reset cones\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The ID/EX, EX/MEM, and MEM/WB registers synchronously reset every payload bit,\\nincluding wide RVFI metadata and data values that are irrelevant while valid is\\nclear. This distributes reset muxing and a high-fanout reset network across\\nhundreds of flops, increasing placement and routing pressure in a design whose\\nfitness is now dominated by preserving its roughly 198 MHz Fmax.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The ID/EX, EX/MEM, and MEM/WB registers synchronously reset every payload bit,\\nincluding wide RVFI metadata and data values that are irrelevant while valid is\\nclear. This distributes reset muxing and a high-fanout reset network across\\nhundreds of flops, increasing placement and routing pressure in a design whose\\nfitness is now dominated by preserving its roughly 198 MHz Fmax.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Reset only each pipeline register's valid bit and the small amount of state that\\ncan act independently of validity, leaving data and RVFI payload fields as\\ndon't-care state until a valid instruction overwrites them. Explicitly qualify\\nredirects, memory requests, hazard detection, and forwarding enables with the\\ncorresponding stage valid bit so stale unreset controls can never cause an\\narchitectural or bus side effect. This should remove broad reset muxes and reset\\nrouting from the inter-stage datapath without adding any 32-bit mux, comparator,\\nor cross-stage bypass, improving Fmax while preserving latency, IPC, RVFI order,\\ntraps, and randomized bus-stall behavior.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Clear only ID/EX valid on reset or flush, and overwrite the complete payload whenever a new instruction is captured.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Remove reset from inert EX/MEM payload fields while clearing valid and valid-qualify redirect and control behavior so stale state cannot escape.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Reset only MEM/WB valid and gate memory requests, misalignment handling, and masks with EX/MEM valid before dropping payload resets.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/zcore.sv\", \"description\": \"Valid-qualify hazard and forwarding control inputs at the top level while preserving the nret=1 ports and RVFI retirement contract.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add reset-release and reset-during-stall tests proving no stale redirect, memory request, register write, trap, or RVFI retirement can occur before fresh valid payloads arrive.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Clear only ID/EX valid on reset or flush, and overwrite the complete payload whenever a new instruction is captured.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Remove reset from inert EX/MEM payload fields while clearing valid and valid-qualify redirect and control behavior so stale state cannot escape.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Reset only MEM/WB valid and gate memory requests, misalignment handling, and masks with EX/MEM valid before dropping payload resets.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/zcore.sv\", \"description\": \"Valid-qualify hazard and forwarding control inputs at the top level while preserving the nret=1 ports and RVFI retirement contract.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add reset-release and reset-during-stall tests proving no stale redirect, memory request, register write, trap, or RVFI retirement can occur before fresh valid payloads arrive.\"}], \"outcome\": \"broken\", \"formal_passed\": false, \"cosim_passed\": false, \"error\": \"cosim_failed: coremark.elf\", \"slot\": 0, \"round_id\": 8}\n26:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-001-r9s0\", \"title\": \"Canonical source-aware load interlock\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The load-use interlock still compares a pending load destination with both raw\\nregister fields of the following instruction. Immediate bits and other unused\\nfields can therefore alias the load destination and insert unnecessary bubbles\\nin CoreMark. Earlier attempts showed that removing those bubbles is only safe\\nwhen hazard detection, forwarding, and RVFI all agree on which sources exist.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The load-use interlock still compares a pending load destination with both raw\\nregister fields of the following instruction. Immediate bits and other unused\\nfields can therefore alias the load destination and insert unnecessary bubbles\\nin CoreMark. Earlier attempts showed that removing those bubbles is only safe\\nwhen hazard detection, forwarding, and RVFI all agree on which sources exist.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Extend the existing decoder with rs1-used and rs2-used outputs, defaulting both\\nfalse and asserting them only in fully validated instruction arms. In ID, use\\nthose bits to canonicalize every unused source address and value to zero before\\nthe ID/EX register, and expose the same canonical combinational addresses to the\\nload-use detector. Thus false raw-field aliases no longer stall, while every\\ntrue dependency retains its bubble and forwarding behavior. Using one decoded\\nsource definition for the interlock and retired RVFI metadata directly addresses\\nthe prior reg_ch0 failure without adding a 32-bit mux or a new cross-stage path,\\nso CoreMark cycles should fall with little effect on the current high Fmax.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/decoder.sv\", \"description\": \"Add default-false rs1-used and rs2-used outputs and assert them only for validated encodings that architecturally consume each source.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Canonicalize unused source addresses and values to zero, using the same decode outputs for regfile reads, ID/EX forwarding metadata, and downstream RVFI.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Consume canonical IF/ID source addresses so load-use detection stalls only for true architectural dependencies.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/zcore.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire ID's canonical combinational source addresses into the hazard unit while preserving all existing bus-stall priorities and the nret=1 interface.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_decoder.py\", \"description\": \"Verify source-use metadata for every legal instruction class plus reserved and illegal encodings.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Check true load dependencies, false aliases in unused fields, canonical RVFI source metadata, and behavior under randomized imem and dmem stalls.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/decoder.sv\", \"description\": \"Add default-false rs1-used and rs2-used outputs and assert them only for validated encodings that architecturally consume each source.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Canonicalize unused source addresses and values to zero, using the same decode outputs for regfile reads, ID/EX forwarding metadata, and downstream RVFI.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Consume canonical IF/ID source addresses so load-use detection stalls only for true architectural dependencies.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/zcore.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire ID's canonical combinational source addresses into the hazard unit while preserving all existing bus-stall priorities and the nret=1 interface.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_decoder.py\", \"description\": \"Verify source-use metadata for every legal instruction class plus reserved and illegal encodings.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Check true load dependencies, false aliases in unused fields, canonical RVFI source metadata, and behavior under randomized imem and dmem stalls.\"}], \"outcome\": \"broken\", \"formal_passed\": false, \"cosim_passed\": false, \"error\": \"cosim_failed: selftest.elf\", \"slot\": 0, \"round_id\": 9}\n29:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-001-r10s0\", \"title\": \"Iterate only cold high-half multiplies\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The current ALU keeps signed and mixed-sign 64-bit multiply results on the\\ncombinational execute datapath even though CoreMark's performance-critical\\nmultiply traffic is dominated by plain MUL. Prior experiments showed that\\ninterlocking every multiply is far too expensive, while sharing all variants\\nthrough extra pre- or post-product logic also reduces Fmax.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current ALU keeps signed and mixed-sign 64-bit multiply results on the\\ncombinational execute datapath even though CoreMark's performance-critical\\nmultiply traffic is dominated by plain MUL. Prior experiments showed that\\ninterlocking every multiply is far too expensive, while sharing all variants\\nthrough extra pre- or post-product logic also reduces Fmax.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Preserve the existing native 32x32 single-cycle path exclusively for MUL, so\\nCoreMark's hot low-half multiplies retain their present latency and direct DSP\\nmapping. Move only MULH, MULHU, and MULHSU to a compact iterative shift-add\\nunit using the divider's proven sticky completion and execute-interlock\\ndiscipline. Removing high-half signedness selection and wide high-product\\nrouting from the normal EX result cone should improve placement and Fmax,\\nwhile the added latency applies only to rare high-half instructions. Keep the\\nRISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS substitutions combinational and preserve exact operands,\\nsignedness, bus-stall priority, and exactly-once RVFI retirement.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/mulh_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a compact iterative shift-add unit for MULH, MULHU, and MULHSU with sticky start/done/accept handshaking and exact signed high-half results.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Retain one direct native-width low-half product for single-cycle MUL, remove real high-half products from the combinational ALU, and leave ALTOPS behavior unchanged.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Dispatch only high-half multiply operations to the iterative unit, capture forwarded RVFI operands, and inject one completed EX/MEM transaction using the existing multi-cycle discipline.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Extend the execute wait interlock to cover high-half multiply completion without changing ordinary MUL, redirect, or memory-backpressure behavior.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/zcore.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire the generalized execute wait path while preserving the nret=1 top-level interface and retirement ordering.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_alu.py\", \"description\": \"Retain direct MUL coverage and verify ALTOPS substitutions after removing real high-half products from the combinational ALU.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add signed boundary, dependent, back-to-back, and randomized-stall tests for iterative MULH variants with exact RVFI results and retirement counts.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/mulh_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a compact iterative shift-add unit for MULH, MULHU, and MULHSU with sticky start/done/accept handshaking and exact signed high-half results.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Retain one direct native-width low-half product for single-cycle MUL, remove real high-half products from the combinational ALU, and leave ALTOPS behavior unchanged.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Dispatch only high-half multiply operations to the iterative unit, capture forwarded RVFI operands, and inject one completed EX/MEM transaction using the existing multi-cycle discipline.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Extend the execute wait interlock to cover high-half multiply completion without changing ordinary MUL, redirect, or memory-backpressure behavior.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/zcore.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire the generalized execute wait path while preserving the nret=1 top-level interface and retirement ordering.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_alu.py\", \"description\": \"Retain direct MUL coverage and verify ALTOPS substitutions after removing real high-half products from the combinational ALU.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add signed boundary, dependent, back-to-back, and randomized-stall tests for iterative MULH variants with exact RVFI results and retirement counts.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 242.49, \"delta_pct\": -45.03, \"vs_baseline\": -14.26, \"fmax_mhz\": 108.92, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4491735, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 5911, \"ff\": 2351, \"seeds\": [113.86, 108.9, 108.92], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-12T00:18:41.805372Z\", \"slot\": 0, \"round_id\": 10, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-001-r10s0 (regression, -45.03%): Making only cold MULH/MULHU/MULHSU iterative still loses when the new shift-add state, completion/result muxing, and pipeline-wide execute interlock add always-present routing cost, so rare dynamic use does not make a separate multi-cycle unit timing-free.\"}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
