{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,240p' cores/bench/LESSONS.md && rg -n '\\\"category\\\"\\\\s*:\\\\s*\\\"structural\\\"|category: structural|structural' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | tail -n 40 && git status --short\"","aggregated_output":"- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-002-r1s1 (improvement, +55.97%): Replacing the combinational DIV/REM cone with a shared radix-2 iterative M-unit greatly improves Fmax and routing efficiency when CoreMark's low dynamic divide frequency amortizes the resulting pipeline freezes.\n- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-001-r2s0 (broken, n/a): Opcode-qualified load-use interlocks must also qualify or zero RVFI unused-source metadata, or raw rs1/rs2 fields can expose stale pre-load values that reg_ch0 rejects even when the instruction does not architecturally consume those operands.\n- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-003-r2s2 (regression, -22.31%): Exact JAL prediction loses overall when J-immediate reconstruction and a 32-bit target adder sit directly on the fetch-to-PC critical path, so direct-target prediction must be staged or kept off the Fmax-limiting next-PC cone.\n- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-002-r3s1 (regression, -37.74%): A zero-latency two-entry fetch skid buffer can lose badly when its queue-head/live-response mux and count/valid control remain on the combinational IF-to-ID path, so limited prefetch during backend holds must amortize the front-end timing and routing cost.\n- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-003-r3s2 (regression, -44.80%): Caching resolved targets does not make prediction timing-free: an asynchronous direct-mapped micro-BTB's tag compare, counter/unconditional decision, and 32-bit target mux on the fetch next-PC cone can overwhelm CoreMark's saved control bubbles, so BTB lookup must be staged or mapped to a timing-friendly memory structure.\n- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-001-r4s0 (regression, -19.79%): Replacing a reset register array with per-entry validity bits can hurt an asynchronous 2R1W register file because indexed valid checks add muxing to both combinational read paths without making its write-first multi-read structure memory-inference-friendly.\n- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-002-r4s1 (regression, -22.95%): Sharing RV32M variants through an opcode-selected signed 33x33 product can hurt FPGA timing because widening the native 32-bit multiply and placing sign/zero-extension muxes before it outweighs eliminating parallel products; prefer native-width multiplication with post-product sign correction.\n- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-003-r4s2 (regression, -48.43%): Registering a micro-BTB lookup is insufficient when the four-entry tag/target/counter flop arrays and widened IF/ID prediction payload add enough placement and routing pressure to outweigh saving one CoreMark taken-control bubble; a staged predictor must also use compact, memory-friendly state and metadata.\n- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-003-r5s2 (regression, -59.29%): An adder-free RAS is not a timing-free return predictor when canonical-JALR decode and a 32-bit registered-stack-top mux still drive the fetch next-PC cone, so CoreMark return bubbles cannot amortize the added stack state and cross-pipeline control routing.\n- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-002-r6s1 (regression, -45.75%): Removing a load-to-store-data bubble can lose badly when the late WB-to-MEM bypass adds a register compare and 32-bit mux directly to store-data/RVFI routing, so even non-ALU forwarding must be kept off timing-sensitive cross-stage paths.\n- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-002-r7s1 (regression, -18.75%): Preselecting ALU-versus-load writeback before MEM/WB can outweigh narrower pipeline records by moving a 32-bit mux onto the load-extension-to-register setup path, so dead-payload pruning and mux relocation should be evaluated separately at high Fmax.\n- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-003-r7s2 (regression, -28.72%): Compressing a one-entry loop predictor to a 10-bit page-local tag and 12-bit target still leaves a compare and target mux on the fetch next-PC cone plus live-entry validation in EX, so CoreMark's saved loop bubbles cannot amortize even narrow combinational prediction unless lookup is staged or removed from timing-critical control paths.\n- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-002-r8s1 (regression, -17.54%): Collapsing MEM/WB into EX/MEM removes flops but also removes the timing boundary before load formatting, writeback selection, regfile commit, and RVFI fanout, so retirement-stage state can be cheaper than exposing the full dmem-to-commit cone in one cycle.\n- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-002-r9s1 (regression, -47.07%): Registering the DSP-backed MUL family loses badly when each CoreMark multiply asserts a pipeline-wide execute interlock, so a multiply stage is worthwhile only if its post-route Fmax gain exceeds the measured cost of those added hold cycles.\n- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-003-r9s2 (regression, -69.90%): A fetch-side fixed-loop predictor must give EX redirects priority and select redirect_target in the next-PC mux; otherwise misprediction recovery merely reloads the speculative or sequential PC and cannot restore architectural control flow.\n- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-001-r10s0 (regression, -45.03%): Making only cold MULH/MULHU/MULHSU iterative still loses when the new shift-add state, completion/result muxing, and pipeline-wide execute interlock add always-present routing cost, so rare dynamic use does not make a separate multi-cycle unit timing-free.\n- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-002-r10s1 (regression, -11.19%): Splitting ctrl_t into stage-specific EX/MEM and MEM/WB records can regress even with writeback timing unchanged because synthesis already prunes dead bundle bits while explicit control repacking and trap qualification add backend logic and disrupt packing.\n- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-003-r10s2 (regression, -8.54%): Reusing the fetch PC incrementer for a fixed backedge is not timing-free because selecting a 32-bit addend turns the constant PC+4 path into a muxed arithmetic cone, while the prediction marker and exact-signature recovery add cross-pipeline control cost that can outweigh one hot loop's saved bubbles.\n3:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-002-r1s1\", \"title\": \"Replace combinational division with an iterative M-unit\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The post-route critical path runs from ID/EX through the synthesized divide/remainder cone into EX/MEM, reaching roughly 7.9 ns and limiting the median Fmax to 127.03 MHz. The same combinational divider also contributes substantial LUT and routing pressure in a design already using 9563 LUT4s, whereas the CoreMark binary contains only a handful of static DIVU sites.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The post-route critical path runs from ID/EX through the synthesized divide/remainder cone into EX/MEM, reaching roughly 7.9 ns and limiting the median Fmax to 127.03 MHz. The same combinational divider also contributes substantial LUT and routing pressure in a design already using 9563 LUT4s, whereas the CoreMark binary contains only a handful of static DIVU sites.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Move DIV, DIVU, REM, and REMU out of the combinational ALU into a shared radix-2 iterative divider. On a divide instruction, capture the forwarded operands and instruction metadata, freeze the younger pipeline and EX/MEM advancement while the unit iterates, then inject the architecturally correct quotient or remainder into EX/MEM exactly once when done. Keep ordinary ALU and DSP-backed multiply instructions single-cycle. Removing the unrolled divider cone should materially raise Fmax and reduce placement congestion; the low dynamic division frequency should make the added divide latency much smaller than the clock-rate gain. Preserve divide-by-zero and signed-overflow semantics, RVFI operands/order, bus-stall priority, redirects, and the RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS behavior.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 15, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/div_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a shared iterative signed/unsigned quotient-remainder unit with start, busy, done, and result handshaking plus all RV32M corner-case semantics.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Remove real combinational divide and remainder operators from the normal hardware datapath while retaining the ALTOPS formal substitutions and single-cycle non-divide operations.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Recognize divide-class operations, capture their forwarded operands and pipeline payload, wait for the iterative result, and emit one completed EX/MEM transaction without duplicate redirects or retirement.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Add multi-cycle execute interlocking that freezes IF and ID, prevents EX/MEM overwrite, and composes correctly with instruction/data bus stalls and redirect flushing.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire the divider busy/completion handshake through execute and hazard control while preserving the nret=1 top-level and RVFI interface.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_alu.py\", \"description\": \"Adapt unit coverage so combinational ALU tests continue to cover non-divide and ALTOPS behavior while division correctness is exercised through the multi-cycle path.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add pipeline tests for dependent and back-to-back DIV/REM operations, corner cases, random imem/dmem stalls, and exactly-once RVFI retirement during execute interlocks.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 15, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/div_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a shared iterative signed/unsigned quotient-remainder unit with start, busy, done, and result handshaking plus all RV32M corner-case semantics.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Remove real combinational divide and remainder operators from the normal hardware datapath while retaining the ALTOPS formal substitutions and single-cycle non-divide operations.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Recognize divide-class operations, capture their forwarded operands and pipeline payload, wait for the iterative result, and emit one completed EX/MEM transaction without duplicate redirects or retirement.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Add multi-cycle execute interlocking that freezes IF and ID, prevents EX/MEM overwrite, and composes correctly with instruction/data bus stalls and redirect flushing.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire the divider busy/completion handshake through execute and hazard control while preserving the nret=1 top-level and RVFI interface.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_alu.py\", \"description\": \"Adapt unit coverage so combinational ALU tests continue to cover non-divide and ALTOPS behavior while division correctness is exercised through the multi-cycle path.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add pipeline tests for dependent and back-to-back DIV/REM operations, corner cases, random imem/dmem stalls, and exactly-once RVFI retirement during execute interlocks.\"}], \"outcome\": \"improvement\", \"fitness\": 441.11, \"delta_pct\": 55.97, \"vs_baseline\": 55.97, \"fmax_mhz\": 198.14, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4491817, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 5376, \"ff\": 2085, \"seeds\": [198.14, 204.92, 197.36], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-11T20:45:07.936221Z\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 1, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-002-r1s1 (improvement, +55.97%): Replacing the combinational DIV/REM cone with a shared radix-2 iterative M-unit greatly improves Fmax and routing efficiency when CoreMark's low dynamic divide frequency amortizes the resulting pipeline freezes.\"}\n6:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-002-r2s1\", \"title\": \"Add a load-result MEM-to-EX bypass\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The pipeline currently inserts a full bubble after every load whose destination\\nmatches either source field of the following instruction, even though the\\nzero-wait data memory returns the load word combinationally in MEM. CoreMark has\\nmany pointer-chasing and structure-access sequences where a load is consumed\\nimmediately, so this conservative interlock directly reduces iterations per\\ncycle; random dmem backpressure already provides the hold mechanism needed when\\nthe returned word is not yet valid.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The pipeline currently inserts a full bubble after every load whose destination\\nmatches either source field of the following instruction, even though the\\nzero-wait data memory returns the load word combinationally in MEM. CoreMark has\\nmany pointer-chasing and structure-access sequences where a load is consumed\\nimmediately, so this conservative interlock directly reduces iterations per\\ncycle; random dmem backpressure already provides the hold mechanism needed when\\nthe returned word is not yet valid.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a true MEM-to-EX result bypass that forwards the extracted and extended load\\nvalue, rather than the EX/MEM address, to the following instruction. Remove the\\nunconditional load-use bubble: if dmem is ready, the dependent instruction can\\nexecute in the same cycle that the load occupies MEM; if dmem is stalled, retain\\nboth the load and its consumer and suppress redirects until the valid load value\\narrives. This structural forwarding path should improve CoreMark IPC enough to\\noutweigh its modest added load-data-to-ALU timing cone, while preserving in-order\\nretirement, exactly-once memory effects, RVFI operand values, and the nret=1\\ninterface.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Expose the current MEM-stage architectural result, selecting the sign/zero-extended load value for loads and the registered ALU result otherwise, as an EX forwarding source.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/forward_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Treat an EX/MEM load as forwardable from the new MEM result path while retaining youngest-writer priority and x0 suppression.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Consume the MEM-stage result bypass and suppress dependent branch or jump redirects while an older memory transaction is held by dmem backpressure.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Remove the fixed load-use bubble and rely on the existing whole-pipeline dmem hold when a forwarded load result is not ready.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/zcore.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire the MEM-stage load result into the EX forwarding input and preserve the existing bus-stall and nret=1 RVFI topology.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed load-to-ALU, load-to-store, and load-to-branch dependency tests with zero-wait and randomized multi-cycle dmem stalls, checking no bubble in the ready case and exactly-once ordered RVFI retirement.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Expose the current MEM-stage architectural result, selecting the sign/zero-extended load value for loads and the registered ALU result otherwise, as an EX forwarding source.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/forward_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Treat an EX/MEM load as forwardable from the new MEM result path while retaining youngest-writer priority and x0 suppression.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Consume the MEM-stage result bypass and suppress dependent branch or jump redirects while an older memory transaction is held by dmem backpressure.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Remove the fixed load-use bubble and rely on the existing whole-pipeline dmem hold when a forwarded load result is not ready.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/zcore.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire the MEM-stage load result into the EX forwarding input and preserve the existing bus-stall and nret=1 RVFI topology.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed load-to-ALU, load-to-store, and load-to-branch dependency tests with zero-wait and randomized multi-cycle dmem stalls, checking no bubble in the ready case and exactly-once ordered RVFI retirement.\"}], \"outcome\": \"broken\", \"formal_passed\": false, \"cosim_passed\": false, \"error\": \"formal_failed: reg_ch0\\nshed (returncode=0)\\nSBY 17:52:21 [insn_sw_ch0] engine_0: Status returned by engine: pass\\nSBY 17:52:21 [insn_sw_ch0] summary: Elapsed clock time [H:MM:SS (secs)]: 0:00:02 (2)\\nSBY 17:52:21 [insn_sw_ch0] summary: Elapsed process time [H:MM:SS (secs)]: 0:00:01 (1)\\nSBY 17:52:21 [insn_sw_ch0] summary: engine_0 (smtbmc bitwuzla) returned pass\\nSBY 17:52:21 [insn_sw_ch0] summary: engine_0 did not produce any traces\\nSBY 17:52:21 [insn_sw_ch0] DONE (PASS, rc=0)\\nSBY 17:52:21 [insn_xori_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:00:01  Status: passed\\nSBY 17:52:21 [insn_xori_ch0] engine_0: finished (returncode=0)\\nSBY 17:52:21 [insn_xori_ch0] engine_0: Status returned by engine: pass\\nSBY 17:52:21 [insn_xori_ch0] summary: Elapsed clock time [H:MM:SS (secs)]: 0:00:02 (2)\\nSBY 17:52:21 [insn_xori_ch0] summary: Elapsed process time [H:MM:SS (secs)]: 0:00:01 (1)\\nSBY 17:52:21 [insn_xori_ch0] summary: engine_0 (smtbmc bitwuzla) returned pass\\nSBY 17:52:21 [insn_xori_ch0] summary: engine_0 did not produce any traces\\nSBY 17:52:21 [insn_xori_ch0] DONE (PASS, rc=0)\\n\\nFormal: 52 passed, 1 failed\\nFailed: reg_ch0\\n\\n--- reg_ch0/logfile.txt (last 30 lines) ---\\nSBY 17:52:08 [reg_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:00:00  Skipping step 9..\\nSBY 17:52:08 [reg_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:00:00  Skipping step 10..\\nSBY 17:52:08 [reg_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:00:00  Skipping step 11..\\nSBY 17:52:08 [reg_ch