nstruction, so it can be predicted exactly without a BTB, history table, or\\naliasing risk. The previous combined JAL and conditional-branch proposal failed\\nduring implementation compilation, making a narrower predictor the best path to\\nretain useful control-flow speedup with much lower RTL and timing risk.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The pipeline currently treats every JAL as an EX-stage redirect, discarding the\\nalready fetched target instruction and paying a control bubble for direct calls\\nand unconditional jumps. A JAL target is encoded entirely in the fetched\\ninstruction, so it can be predicted exactly without a BTB, history table, or\\naliasing risk. The previous combined JAL and conditional-branch proposal failed\\nduring implementation compilation, making a narrower predictor the best path to\\nretain useful control-flow speedup with much lower RTL and timing risk.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Decode only the JAL opcode and J-immediate in IF, select pc+imm as the predicted\\nnext fetch address, and carry that predicted next PC to EX. EX will compare the\\narchitecturally resolved next PC against the prediction and redirect/flush only\\non a mismatch, including recovery to pc+4 for a misaligned JAL trap. All other\\ninstructions remain predict-not-taken and preserve the existing recovery path.\\nThis stateless, always-correct prediction should remove the EX redirect penalty\\nfor every aligned direct call and jump while adding only a small immediate adder\\nand metadata register, so iterations per cycle should rise with little Fmax loss.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add predicted-next-PC metadata to the IF/ID and ID/EX payloads.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Recognize fetched JAL instructions, form their J-immediate target, advance the PC to that target, and emit the selected predicted next PC while respecting stalls and recovery priority.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Latch predicted-next-PC metadata with the instruction into ID/EX.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Generate a recovery redirect only when resolved next PC differs from the carried prediction, including misaligned-JAL trap recovery, while keeping architectural RVFI pc_wdata unchanged.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire the EX recovery indication and target through the existing IF and hazard redirect paths without changing the nret=1 interface.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed checks that aligned JAL reaches its target without a recovery bubble and that misaligned JAL traps, suppresses rd writeback, and recovers to pc+4 under bus stalls.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add predicted-next-PC metadata to the IF/ID and ID/EX payloads.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Recognize fetched JAL instructions, form their J-immediate target, advance the PC to that target, and emit the selected predicted next PC while respecting stalls and recovery priority.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Latch predicted-next-PC metadata with the instruction into ID/EX.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Generate a recovery redirect only when resolved next PC differs from the carried prediction, including misaligned-JAL trap recovery, while keeping architectural RVFI pc_wdata unchanged.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire the EX recovery indication and target through the existing IF and hazard redirect paths without changing the nret=1 interface.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed checks that aligned JAL reaches its target without a recovery bubble and that misaligned JAL traps, suppresses rd writeback, and recovers to pc+4 under bus stalls.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 342.69, \"delta_pct\": -22.31, \"vs_baseline\": 21.17, \"fmax_mhz\": 152.23, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4442158, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 5500, \"ff\": 2090, \"seeds\": [145.94, 152.23, 166.64], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-11T20:54:42.482779Z\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 2, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-003-r2s2 (regression, -22.31%): Exact JAL prediction loses overall when J-immediate reconstruction and a 32-bit target adder sit directly on the fetch-to-PC critical path, so direct-target prediction must be staged or kept off the Fmax-limiting next-PC cone.\"}\ncores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:8:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-001-r3s0\", \"title\": \"Eliminate false load-use stalls with canonical source metadata\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The load-use interlock compares a pending load destination with both raw source\\nbit fields of the following instruction, even when its opcode does not consume\\none or both fields. CoreMark's immediate, load, upper-immediate, jump, fence,\\nand system instructions can therefore incur false one-cycle bubbles whenever\\nimmediate bits alias the load destination. The earlier attempt established that\\nremoving those bubbles also requires canonical RVFI metadata for unused sources.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The load-use interlock compares a pending load destination with both raw source\\nbit fields of the following instruction, even when its opcode does not consume\\none or both fields. CoreMark's immediate, load, upper-immediate, jump, fence,\\nand system instructions can therefore incur false one-cycle bubbles whenever\\nimmediate bits alias the load destination. The earlier attempt established that\\nremoving those bubbles also requires canonical RVFI metadata for unused sources.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Decode rs1-used and rs2-used qualifiers once in ID, drive unused regfile read\\naddresses to x0, and carry zero source addresses and values into ID/EX for RVFI.\\nFeed those same canonical addresses to the load-use detector, so it stalls only\\non an architecturally consumed register. This should recover cycles without\\nadding a new datapath cone, while the zeroed metadata preserves formal register\\ntracking, forwarding behavior, bus-stall priority, and retirement semantics.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"high\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/decoder.sv\", \"description\": \"Emit validated rs1-used and rs2-used qualifiers for every legal RV32IM instruction class, defaulting both low for illegal and source-free encodings.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Canonicalize unused source addresses and values to zero for regfile reads, ID/EX forwarding metadata, and downstream RVFI reporting.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Use the canonical IF/ID source addresses so the load-use interlock bubbles only for true architectural dependencies.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/zcore.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire ID's canonical combinational source addresses into hazard detection while preserving the nret=1 interface and existing stall priorities.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_decoder.py\", \"description\": \"Check source-use qualifiers across R, I, load/store, branch, jump, upper-immediate, fence, EBREAK, and illegal encodings.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Verify true load consumers still stall, false raw-field aliases do not, and unused RVFI source addresses and data retire as zero under bus stalls.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"high\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/decoder.sv\", \"description\": \"Emit validated rs1-used and rs2-used qualifiers for every legal RV32IM instruction class, defaulting both low for illegal and source-free encodings.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Canonicalize unused source addresses and values to zero for regfile reads, ID/EX forwarding metadata, and downstream RVFI reporting.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Use the canonical IF/ID source addresses so the load-use interlock bubbles only for true architectural dependencies.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/zcore.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire ID's canonical combinational source addresses into hazard detection while preserving the nret=1 interface and existing stall priorities.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_decoder.py\", \"description\": \"Check source-use qualifiers across R, I, load/store, branch, jump, upper-immediate, fence, EBREAK, and illegal encodings.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Verify true load consumers still stall, false raw-field aliases do not, and unused RVFI source addresses and data retire as zero under bus stalls.\"}], \"outcome\": \"broken\", \"formal_passed\": false, \"cosim_passed\": false, \"error\": \"cosim_failed: selftest.elf\", \"slot\": 0, \"round_id\": 3}\ncores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:9:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-002-r3s1\", \"title\": \"Add a fall-through instruction skid buffer\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current fetch stage couples the program counter directly to decode, so every\\ncycle with io_imemReady low injects a bubble even when the back end previously\\nstalled for a load-use dependency, data-memory backpressure, or an iterative\\ndivide. Those back-end hold cycles are opportunities to fetch one or two\\nsequential instructions ahead and turn later random instruction-memory stalls\\ninto useful execution cycles. The failed direct-JAL predictor also showed that\\nfetch-path arithmetic must remain off the Fmax-critical next-PC cone.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current fetch stage couples the program counter directly to decode, so every\\ncycle with io_imemReady low injects a bubble even when the back end previously\\nstalled for a load-use dependency, data-memory backpressure, or an iterative\\ndivide. Those back-end hold cycles are opportunities to fetch one or two\\nsequential instructions ahead and turn later random instruction-memory stalls\\ninto useful execution cycles. The failed direct-JAL predictor also showed that\\nfetch-path arithmetic must remain off the Fmax-critical next-PC cone.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace the rigid fetch-to-decode connection with a two-entry fall-through\\ninstruction skid buffer holding {pc, instruction}. When the queue is empty and\\nmemory is ready, preserve the existing combinational fall-through path so an\\nordinary instruction gains no extra latency; while decode is held, advance a\\nseparate sequential fetch pointer and fill the two entries. Decode consumes the\\noldest entry independently of transient io_imemReady deassertions. A redirect\\nmust atomically discard all buffered wrong-path entries, set the fetch pointer to\\nthe resolved target, and retain priority over every hold condition. This should\\nrecover IPC under the benchmark's random instruction stalls using only shallow\\nregisters and simple valid-bit control, with little Fmax or LUT cost and without\\nrecreating the predictor's target-adder critical path.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Implement a two-entry fall-through instruction queue, an independent sequential fetch pointer, enqueue/dequeue handshakes, and redirect-time invalidation with redirect priority.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Separate front-end response availability from back-end decode holds so an imem miss stalls consumption only when the skid buffer has no valid instruction, while back-end holds allow prefetch.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/zcore.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire fetch-queue valid/consume control through the existing single-retire pipeline without changing the nret=1 top-level IO or RVFI ordering.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed and randomized checks for fall-through, queue fill during load-use/dmem/divide holds, queue-empty imem stalls, full-queue backpressure, and redirect flushing under simultaneous imem/dmem stalls.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Implement a two-entry fall-through instruction queue, an independent sequential fetch pointer, enqueue/dequeue handshakes, and redirect-time invalidation with redirect priority.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Separate front-end response availability from back-end decode holds so an imem miss stalls consumption only when the skid buffer has no valid instruction, while back-end holds allow prefetch.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/zcore.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire fetch-queue valid/consume control through the existing single-retire pipeline without changing the nret=1 top-level IO or RVFI ordering.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed and randomized checks for fall-through, queue fill during load-use/dmem/divide holds, queue-empty imem stalls, full-queue backpressure, and redirect flushing under simultaneous imem/dmem stalls.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 274.62, \"delta_pct\": -37.74, \"vs_baseline\": -2.9, \"fmax_mhz\": 119.23, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4341607, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 5519, \"ff\": 2209, \"seeds\": [109.05, 126.23, 119.23], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-11T21:06:42.849489Z\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 3, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-002-r3s1 (regression, -37.74%): A zero-latency two-entry fetch skid buffer can lose badly when its queue-head/live-response mux and count/valid control remain on the combinational IF-to-ID path, so limited prefetch during backend holds must amortize the front-end timing and routing cost.\"}\ncores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:10:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-003-r3s2\", \"title\": \"Target-cached micro-BTB with hysteresis\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"CoreMark repeatedly executes taken loop branches, calls, returns, and other jumps,\\nbut the current core waits until EX to redirect every one and discards younger\\nwork. The prior exact-JAL predictor proved that putting immediate reconstruction\\nand a 32-bit target adder in the fetch next-PC cone costs more Fmax than it saves\\nin control bubbles. A learned target cache can recover the same control-flow\\nopportunities without recomputing a target in fetch.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"CoreMark repeatedly executes taken loop branches, calls, returns, and other jumps,\\nbut the current core waits until EX to redirect every one and discards younger\\nwork. The prior exact-JAL predictor proved that putting immediate reconstruction\\nand a 32-bit target adder in the fetch next-PC cone costs more Fmax than it saves\\nin control bubbles. A learned target cache can recover the same control-flow\\nopportunities without recomputing a target in fetch.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a small direct-mapped micro-BTB trained by resolved control instructions in\\nEX. Each entry holds a valid bit, a PC tag, the already-computed target, an\\nunconditional flag, and a 2-bit saturating direction counter for conditional\\nbranches. Fetch predicts only on a valid tag hit and selects the cached target;\\notherwise it remains sequential. Carry the predicted next PC with the instruction\\nand make EX flush and redirect only when the architectural next PC differs. This\\nshould remove repeated loop, JAL, JALR, and return penalties while keeping J/B\\nimmediate decoding and target addition entirely off the fetch critical path.\\nA modest table with a shallow low-PC index should limit LUT, fanout, and routing\\ncost; tags prevent destructive alias predictions, and normal EX recovery preserves\\ncorrectness under cold misses, direction changes, replacement, and bus stalls.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add predicted-next-PC metadata to the IF/ID and ID/EX payloads so each control instruction can be checked against the exact fetch decision.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a small direct-mapped tagged BTB with cached targets, unconditional classification, and 2-bit branch counters; predict from a tag hit without a fetch-time target adder and update entries from EX resolution.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Pipeline the predicted next PC alongside the instruction while preserving existing flush and stall behavior.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Export resolved control-flow training information and generate recovery only when the resolved architectural next PC differs from the carried prediction, including not-taken and misalignment recovery.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/zcore.sv\", \"description\": \"Connect BTB training and misprediction recovery between EX and IF without changing the nret=1 top-level interface or RVFI retirement path.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed repeated-loop, JAL, JALR-return, direction-change, alias-replacement, misalignment, and randomized bus-stall tests checking prediction recovery and ordered RVFI behavior.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add predicted-next-PC metadata to the IF/ID and ID/EX payloads so each control instruction can be checked against the exact fetch decision.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a small direct-mapped tagged BTB with cached targets, unconditional classification, and 2-bit branch counters; predict from a tag hit without a fetch-time target adder and update entries from EX resolution.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Pipeline the predicted next PC alongside the instruction while preserving existing flush and stall behavior.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Export resolved control-flow training information and generate recovery only when the resolved architectural next PC differs from the carried prediction, including not-taken and misalignment recovery.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/zcore.sv\", \"description\": \"Connect BTB training and misprediction recovery between EX and IF without changing the nret=1 top-level interface or RVFI retirement path.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed repeated-loop, JAL, JALR-return, direction-change, alias-replacement, misalignment, and randomized bus-stall tests checking prediction recovery and ordered RVFI behavior.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 243.48, \"delta_pct\": -44.8, \"vs_baseline\": -13.91, \"fmax_mhz\": 103.79, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4262695, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 6710, \"ff\": 2605, \"seeds\": [103.79, 108.45, 101.98], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-11T21:11:59.759048Z\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 3, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-003-r3s2 (regression, -44.80%): Caching resolved targets does not make prediction timing-free: an asynchronous direct-mapped micro-BTB's tag compare, counter/unconditional decision, and 32-bit target mux on the fetch next-PC cone can overwhelm CoreMark's saved control bubbles, so BTB lookup must be staged or mapped to a timing-friendly memory structure.\"}\ncores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:12:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-002-r4s1\", \"title\": \"Share one mixed-sign multiplier datapath\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The combinational ALU currently constructs three independent 64-bit products\\nfor signed-signed, unsigned-unsigned, and signed-unsigned multiplication on\\nevery cycle. Even though only one MUL-class result can be selected, this\\nduplicates wide arithmetic and its associated DSP/LUT routing. After the\\niterative divider removed the dominant divide cone, multiplier duplication is\\na promising remaining source of placement pressure and Fmax loss.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The combinational ALU currently constructs three independent 64-bit products\\nfor signed-signed, unsigned-unsigned, and signed-unsigned multiplication on\\nevery cycle. Even though only one MUL-class result can be selected, this\\nduplicates wide arithmetic and its associated DSP/LUT routing. After the\\niterative divider removed the dominant divide cone, multiplier duplication is\\na promising remaining source of placement pressure and Fmax loss.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace the three parallel products with one shared widened signed multiplier.\\nSelectively sign- or zero-extend each operand to 33 bits according to MULH,\\nMULHU, or MULHSU, compute one signed 33-by-33 product, and select the required\\nlow or high 32-bit slice. Plain MUL may use zero-extended operands because the\\nlow 32 product bits are independent of signedness. This preserves single-cycle\\nmultiply latency and CoreMark IPC while reducing duplicated multiplier logic,\\nDSP demand, and wide-result routing, which should improve post-route Fmax. Keep\\nthe RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS substitutions unchanged and verify all signedness and\\nboundary cases against exact RV32M results.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 6, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the three parallel 64-bit products with operand-signedness selection feeding one shared widened multiplier, retaining the existing ALTOPS branch and output semantics.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_alu.py\", \"description\": \"Expand directed and randomized MUL, MULH, MULHU, and MULHSU vectors to prove low-half equivalence and correct high-half signedness at zero, sign-boundary, and all-ones operands.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 6, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the three parallel 64-bit products with operand-signedness selection feeding one shared widened multiplier, retaining the existing ALTOPS branch and output semantics.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_alu.py\", \"description\": \"Expand directed and randomized MUL, MULH, MULHU, and MULHSU vectors to prove low-half equivalence and correct high-half signedness at zero, sign-boundary, and all-ones operands.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 339.88, \"delta_pct\": -22.95, \"vs_baseline\": 20.18, \"fmax_mhz\": 152.67, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4491817, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 5377, \"ff\": 2081, \"seeds\": [149.1, 161.06, 152.67], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-11T21:19:57.623120Z\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 4, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-002-r4s1 (regression, -22.95%): Sharing RV32M variants through an opcode-selected signed 33x33 product can hurt FPGA timing because widening the native 32-bit multiply and placing sign/zero-extension muxes before it outweighs eliminating parallel products; prefer native-width multiplication with post-product sign correction.\"}\ncores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:13:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-003-r4s2\", \"title\": \"Pipeline the micro-BTB lookup\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"CoreMark repeatedly executes taken loop branches and jumps, so resolving every\\ncontrol transfer in EX wastes front-end cycles. The two working predictor\\nattempts regressed because target generation or an asynchronous BTB lookup fed\\nthe fetch PC in the same cycle, collapsing FPGA Fmax despite modest IPC gains.\\nThe prior results indicate that predictor timing, rather than prediction value,\\nis the limiting problem.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"CoreMark repeatedly executes taken loop branches and jumps, so resolving every\\ncontrol transfer in EX wastes front-end cycles. The two working predictor\\nattempts regressed because target generation or an asynchronous BTB lookup fed\\nthe fetch PC in the same cycle, collapsing FPGA Fmax despite modest IPC gains.\\nThe prior results indicate that predictor timing, rather than prediction value,\\nis the limiting problem.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a very small tagged BTB whose lookup result is registered before it can\\nredirect fetch. On cycle N the current PC indexes the table while fetch proceeds\\nsequentially; at the clock edge the hit, cached target, direction decision, and\\nlookup PC are captured. On cycle N+1 a valid registered prediction redirects to\\nthe cached target and squashes only the single sequential instruction fetched\\nbehind it. EX trains the table and still performs exact recovery whenever the\\nresolved architectural next PC differs from the prediction carried with the\\ncontrol instruction. This deliberately one-cycle-late predictor saves one of\\nthe existing taken-control bubbles while keeping tag comparison, counter decode,\\ntarget storage, and immediate arithmetic off the same-cycle PC next-state cone.\\nA four-entry table and registered prediction outputs should preserve most of the\\ncurrent 198 MHz placement quality, making a smaller IPC gain worthwhile on the\\nfitness product.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Carry the fetch prediction identity and predicted next PC through IF/ID and ID/EX so EX can validate the delayed decision precisely.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Implement a four-entry tagged BTB with registered lookup outputs, one-cycle-late target redirect, single-instruction squash, EX training, and redirect/stall priority that never places table lookup logic on the same-cycle PC cone.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Latch delayed-prediction metadata with the associated control instruction and preserve it across pipeline holds.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Export BTB training data and recover only when the resolved next PC disagrees with the prediction associated with the instruction, including not-taken and misalignment cases.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/zcore.sv\", \"description\": \"Connect registered BTB prediction, EX training, and misprediction recovery while retaining the nret=1 top-level and ordered RVFI path.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Distinguish the delayed predictor's one-instruction squash from an EX misprediction flush and preserve existing bus and divide hold priorities.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add repeated-loop, jump, direction-change, alias-replacement, and simultaneous bus-stall tests that verify the delayed prediction saves one bubble and always recovers with exact ordered RVFI retirement.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Carry the fetch prediction identity and predicted next PC through IF/ID and ID/EX so EX can validate the delayed decision precisely.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Implement a four-entry tagged BTB with registered lookup outputs, one-cycle-late target redirect, single-instruction squash, EX training, and redirect/stall priority that never places table lookup logic on the same-cycle PC cone.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Latch delayed-prediction metadata with the associated control instruction and preserve it across pipeline holds.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Export BTB training data and recover only when the resolved next PC disagrees with the prediction associated with the instruction, including not-taken and misalignment cases.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/zcore.sv\", \"description\": \"Connect registered BTB prediction, EX training, and misprediction recovery while retaining the nret=1 top-level and ordered RVFI path.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Distinguish the delayed predictor's one-instruction squash from an EX misprediction flush and preserve existing bus and divide hold priorities.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add repeated-loop, jump, direction-change, alias-replacement, and simultaneous bus-stall tests that verify the delayed prediction saves one bubble and always recovers with exact ordered RVFI retirement.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 227.47, \"delta_pct\": -48.43, \"vs_baseline\": -19.57, \"fmax_mhz\": 105.29, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4628748, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 6110, \"ff\": 2491, \"seeds\": [102.5, 105.29, 106.78], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-11T21:27:35.729641Z\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 4, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-003-r4s2 (regression, -48.43%): Registering a micro-BTB lookup is insufficient when the four-entry tag/target/counter flop arrays and widened IF/ID prediction payload add enough placement and routing pressure to outweigh saving one CoreMark taken-control bubble; a staged predictor must also use compact, memory-friendly state and metadata.\"}\ncores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:15:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-002-r5s1\", \"title\": \"RVFI-safe load-result bypass\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The pipeline still inserts a bubble whenever a load is followed by an\\ninstruction whose raw rs1 or rs2 field matches the load destination, even\\nthough a ready data memory produces the extended load value combinationally\\nin MEM. CoreMark contains dense pointer and structure-access chains, so this\\nfixed load-use penalty directly reduces iterations per cycle. The earlier\\nattempt at this structure failed formal because unused encoded source fields\\nand their RVFI metadata were not qualified; it therefore supplied no fitness\\nevidence against the bypass itself.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The pipeline still inserts a bubble whenever a load is followed by an\\ninstruction whose raw rs1 or rs2 field matches the load destination, even\\nthough a ready data memory produces the extended load value combinationally\\nin MEM. CoreMark contains dense pointer and structure-access chains, so this\\nfixed load-use penalty directly reduces iterations per cycle. The earlier\\nattempt at this structure failed formal because unused encoded source fields\\nand their RVFI metadata were not qualified; it therefore supplied no fitness\\nevidence against the bypass itself.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add explicit uses_rs1 and uses_rs2 control metadata at decode, carry it to\\nretirement, and use it consistently for hazard comparisons, forwarding, and\\nRVFI source address/data reporting. In parallel, expose the current MEM-stage\\narchitectural result and forward the sign- or zero-extended load value to the\\ndependent instruction in EX. Eliminate the fixed load-use bubble when dmem is\\nready; existing whole-pipeline backpressure continues to hold both producer\\nand consumer while dmem is not ready. This should recover one cycle from\\ncommon load-consumer pairs without changing the pipeline depth, retirement\\nwidth, or memory protocol, while the explicit source-use contract closes the\\nformal failure mode from the prior attempt.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add uses_rs1 and uses_rs2 bits to the pipeline control metadata so architectural source validity is preserved through retirement.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/decoder.sv\", \"description\": \"Assert source-use bits only inside validated instruction forms, distinguishing true operands from immediate and otherwise unused encoded fields while retaining default-illegal behavior.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Expose the current MEM-stage forwarding result, selecting the extracted and extended load data for loads and the registered ALU result for other producers.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/forward_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Select the MEM-stage architectural result for an EX/MEM dependency and qualify comparisons with the consumer's decoded source-use bits.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Consume the MEM result bypass for dependent ALU, store-data, branch, and JALR operands while preserving dmem-stall holds and redirect suppression.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace raw-field load-use detection with source-qualified matching and remove the fixed bubble because dmem backpressure now gates load-result availability.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/zcore.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire source-use metadata and the MEM-to-EX result path, and zero RVFI source address/data fields for operands the retiring instruction does not architecturally read.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed load-to-ALU, load-to-store, load-to-branch, and false-dependency cases under ready and randomized stalled dmem, checking ordered exactly-once retirement and source-qualified RVFI fields.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add uses_rs1 and uses_rs2 bits to the pipeline control metadata so architectural source validity is preserved through retirement.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/decoder.sv\", \"description\": \"Assert source-use bits only inside validated instruction forms, distinguishing true operands from immediate and otherwise unused encoded fields while retaining default-illegal behavior.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Expose the current MEM-stage forwarding result, selecting the extracted and extended load data for loads and the registered ALU result for other producers.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/forward_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Select the MEM-stage architectural result for an EX/MEM dependency and qualify comparisons with the consumer's decoded source-use bits.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Consume the MEM result bypass for dependent ALU, store-data, branch, and JALR operands while preserving dmem-stall holds and redirect suppression.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace raw-field load-use detection with source-qualified matching and remove the fixed bubble because dmem backpressure now gates load-result availability.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/zcore.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire source-use metadata and the MEM-to-EX result path, and zero RVFI source address/data fields for operands the retiring instruction does not architecturally read.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed load-to-ALU, load-to-store, load-to-branch, and false-dependency cases under ready and randomized stalled dmem, checking ordered exactly-once retirement and source-qualified RVFI fields.\"}], \"outcome\": \"broken\", \"formal_passed\": false, \"cosim_passed\": false, \"error\": \"cosim_failed: selftest.elf\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 5}\ncores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:16:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-003-r5s2\", \"title\": \"Adder-free return-address stack predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"CoreMark contains many function returns, and the current pipeline pays the full\\nEX-stage redirect penalty for every JALR. Prior predictors reduced control-flow\\ncycles but lost much more Fmax to fetch-time target adders, PC-indexed tag arrays,\\ncounters, and wide prediction metadata. Canonical returns offer a narrower target:\\ntheir destination was already computed as the link address of an earlier call.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"CoreMark contains many function returns, and the current pipeline pays the full\\nEX-stage redirect penalty for every JALR. Prior predictors reduced control-flow\\ncycles but lost much more Fmax to fetch-time target adders, PC-indexed tag arrays,\\ncounters, and wide prediction metadata. Canonical returns offer a narrower target:\\ntheir destination was already computed as the link address of an earlier call.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a small return-address stack trained only by resolved calls and returns, and\\npredict only canonical return encodings such as JALR x0, 0(x1) or x5. Fetch selects\\nthe already-registered stack top, requiring no immediate reconstruction, target\\nadder, PC tag comparison, direction counter, or indexed target array. Carry only a\\none-bit predicted-return marker into EX; keep the stack top stable until that return\\nresolves, compare it with the architectural JALR target, and recover normally on a\\nmismatch, underflow, overflow alias, modified link register, or misalignment. A\\nshallow stack should remove a useful class of repeated indirect-control bubbles\\nwhile avoiding the timing and routing structures that caused every prior predictor\\nregression.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a single predicted-return bit to the IF/ID and ID/EX payloads without carrying tags or 32-bit predicted targets through the pipeline.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Implement a shallow return-address stack, recognize only canonical return encodings, select its registered top as the predicted PC, and preserve redirect and bus-stall priority.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Pipeline the one-bit return-prediction marker with the associated instruction and clear it on existing flush paths.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Classify resolved calls and returns for stack updates, suppress the normal JALR redirect only when a marked return matches the current stack top, and issue exact recovery on mismatch or misalignment.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/zcore.sv\", \"description\": \"Connect return-stack training, stack-top validation, and recovery between EX and IF while preserving the nret=1 interface and RVFI retirement behavior.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add nested-call, canonical-return, link-register-modification, underflow, overflow, misalignment, and randomized bus-stall tests for prediction and exact recovery.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a single predicted-return bit to the IF/ID and ID/EX payloads without carrying tags or 32-bit predicted targets through the pipeline.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Implement a shallow return-address stack, recognize only canonical return encodings, select its registered top as the predicted PC, and preserve redirect and bus-stall priority.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Pipeline the one-bit return-prediction marker with the associated instruction and clear it on existing flush paths.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Classify resolved calls and returns for stack updates, suppress the normal JALR redirect only when a marked return matches the current stack top, and issue exact recovery on mismatch or misalignment.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/zcore.sv\", \"description\": \"Connect return-stack training, stack-top validation, and recovery between EX and IF while preserving the nret=1 interface and RVFI retirement behavior.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add nested-call, canonical-return, link-register-modification, underflow, overflow, misalignment, and randomized bus-stall tests for prediction and exact recovery.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 179.59, \"delta_pct\": -59.29, \"vs_baseline\": -36.5, \"fmax_mhz\": 80.42, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4478102, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 5683, \"ff\": 2152, \"seeds\": [85.9, 79.31, 80.42], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-11T21:47:47.624221Z\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 5, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-003-r5s2 (regression, -59.29%): An adder-free RAS is not a timing-free return predictor when canonical-JALR decode and a 32-bit registered-stack-top mux still drive the fetch next-PC cone, so CoreMark return bubbles cannot amortize the added stack state and cross-pipeline control routing.\"}\ncores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:18:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-002-r6s1\", \"title\": \"Add a late load-to-store-data bypass\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current load-use detector stalls a store when an immediately preceding\\nload writes the store's rs2 field, even though store data is not consumed\\nuntil the following MEM stage. CoreMark's structure and list manipulation\\nproduces load-then-store value transfers, so these conservative bubbles cost\\nIPC. The broader MEM-to-EX load bypass attempts never reached fitness and\\nwould place memory data on timing-sensitive ALU and control-flow paths.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current load-use detector stalls a store when an immediately preceding\\nload writes the store's rs2 field, even though store data is not consumed\\nuntil the following MEM stage. CoreMark's structure and list manipulation\\nproduces load-then-store value transfers, so these conservative bubbles cost\\nIPC. The broader MEM-to-EX load bypass attempts never reached fitness and\\nwould place memory data on timing-sensitive ALU and control-flow paths.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Let a store whose data depends on the immediately preceding load advance\\nwithout a load-use bubble, while retaining the stall for a dependency on the\\nstore address. Carry the store's rs2 register identity to MEM and select the\\nretiring load's extended WB value there when the register numbers match.\\nSuppress the existing EX/MEM address forwarding for this specific load-to-\\nstore-data pair so stale or address data cannot enter the store payload.\\nBecause the new mux drives only store data and RVFI metadata, not the ALU,\\nbranch comparator, or fetch PC, it should recover useful cycles with far less\\nFmax risk than a general load-result bypass. Data-bus stalls must continue to\\nhold both operations, and the store must issue and retire exactly once.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Recognize stores in IF/ID and exempt only their rs2 data dependency from the load-use interlock, while preserving stalls for rs1 address dependencies and all non-store consumers.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/forward_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Prevent an EX/MEM load's address result from being selected as the EX-stage rs2 value for the dependent store-data case, leaving other forwarding priorities unchanged.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a late WB-to-store-data comparison and mux, use the selected value for byte-lane replication, and report the same architectural rs2 value and memory write data through RVFI.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/zcore.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire store classification, load classification, and WB register/value signals into the hazard, forwarding, and MEM-stage late-bypass paths without changing the nret=1 interface.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add load-to-store-data and load-to-store-address tests for byte, halfword, and word stores under zero-wait and randomized dmem stalls, checking bubble behavior, exact memory effects, RVFI operands, and ordered retirement.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Recognize stores in IF/ID and exempt only their rs2 data dependency from the load-use interlock, while preserving stalls for rs1 address dependencies and all non-store consumers.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/forward_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Prevent an EX/MEM load's address result from being selected as the EX-stage rs2 value for the dependent store-data case, leaving other forwarding priorities unchanged.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a late WB-to-store-data comparison and mux, use the selected value for byte-lane replication, and report the same architectural rs2 value and memory write data through RVFI.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/zcore.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire store classification, load classification, and WB register/value signals into the hazard, forwarding, and MEM-stage late-bypass paths without changing the nret=1 interface.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add load-to-store-data and load-to-store-address tests for byte, halfword, and word stores under zero-wait and randomized dmem stalls, checking bubble behavior, exact memory effects, RVFI operands, and ordered retirement.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 239.31, \"delta_pct\": -45.75, \"vs_baseline\": -15.38, \"fmax_mhz\": 107.46, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4490382, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 5397, \"ff\": 2066, \"seeds\": [107.46, 112.54, 100.07], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-11T22:01:28.841034Z\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 6, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-002-r6s1 (regression, -45.75%): Removing a load-to-store-data bubble can lose badly when the late WB-to-MEM bypass adds a register compare and 32-bit mux directly to store-data/RVFI routing, so even non-ALU forwarding must be kept off timing-sensitive cross-stage paths.\"}\ncores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:19:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-003-r6s2\", \"title\": \"One-entry conditional loop target buffer\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The baseline pays a redirect bubble for every taken conditional loop branch, but\\nall broader predictor attempts lost far more Fmax than their IPC gain because\\nthey added target arrays, counters, multi-entry selection, wide metadata, or\\nJALR/RAS routing. CoreMark's hot counted loops offer a much narrower opportunity:\\nthe same direct conditional branch and fixed target repeat many times before the\\nloop exit.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The baseline pays a redirect bubble for every taken conditional loop branch, but\\nall broader predictor attempts lost far more Fmax than their IPC gain because\\nthey added target arrays, counters, multi-entry selection, wide metadata, or\\nJALR/RAS routing. CoreMark's hot counted loops offer a much narrower opportunity:\\nthe same direct conditional branch and fixed target repeat many times before the\\nloop exit.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a single-entry last-taken conditional-branch target buffer containing only a\\nvalid bit, the full branch PC, and its resolved target. A full-PC hit predicts the\\nbranch taken; a resolved taken conditional branch installs the entry, while a\\nresolved not-taken instance invalidates a matching entry. Restricting prediction\\nto direct conditional branches makes the cached target immutable for that PC, so\\nonly a one-bit predicted-taken marker must travel to EX. EX recovers on a cold\\ntaken branch or a predicted-taken loop exit and otherwise suppresses the existing\\nredirect. This should capture repeated loop backedges after one warm-up miss with\\nmuch less timing and placement cost than the failed multi-entry BTBs: there is no\\nindexed array, direction counter, unconditional-control decode, JALR path, or\\n32-bit predicted-next-PC pipeline payload.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add only a predicted-taken bit to the IF/ID and ID/EX payloads, avoiding a wide predicted-target metadata path.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Implement the one-entry full-tag conditional loop target buffer, predict only on exact hits, and update or invalidate it from resolved conditional branches with recovery and bus-stall priority.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Latch the one-bit prediction marker with its instruction and clear it on existing flush paths.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Train the entry from resolved conditional branches and generate recovery only when actual direction disagrees with the carried prediction, preserving misalignment traps and architectural pc_wdata.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Treat EX branch-prediction recovery as the existing redirect flush while preserving load, divide, and memory-backpressure priorities.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/zcore.sv\", \"description\": \"Connect conditional-branch training and misprediction recovery between EX and IF without changing the nret=1 top-level or RVFI retirement path.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add repeated-loop warm-up, taken-hit, loop-exit invalidation, tag-miss, misaligned-target, and randomized bus-stall tests with exact ordered RVFI checks.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add only a predicted-taken bit to the IF/ID and ID/EX payloads, avoiding a wide predicted-target metadata path.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Implement the one-entry full-tag conditional loop target buffer, predict only on exact hits, and update or invalidate it from resolved conditional branches with recovery and bus-stall priority.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Latch the one-bit prediction marker with its instruction and clear it on existing flush paths.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Train the entry from resolved conditional branches and generate recovery only when actual direction disagrees with the carried prediction, preserving misalignment traps and architectural pc_wdata.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Treat EX branch-prediction recovery as the existing redirect flush while preserving load, divide, and memory-backpressure priorities.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/zcore.sv\", \"description\": \"Connect conditional-branch training and misprediction recovery between EX and IF without changing the nret=1 top-level or RVFI retirement path.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add repeated-loop warm-up, taken-hit, loop-exit invalidation, tag-miss, misaligned-target, and randomized bus-stall tests with exact ordered RVFI checks.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 360.87, \"delta_pct\": -18.19, \"vs_baseline\": 27.6, \"fmax_mhz\": 155.93, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4320996, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 5670, \"ff\": 2129, \"seeds\": [182.62, 149.75, 155.93], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-11T22:03:30.810290Z\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 6}\ncores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:20:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-001-r7s0\", \"title\": \"Replace register-file reset with FPGA initialization\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The 32x32 register-file data array currently places all 1,024 storage bits on\\nthe synchronous reset network. The previous validity-bitmap experiment showed\\nthat removing this bulk reset can greatly reduce storage cost, but its indexed\\nvalid checks added muxing to both asynchronous read paths and reduced Fmax.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The 32x32 register-file data array currently places all 1,024 storage bits on\\nthe synchronous reset network. The previous validity-bitmap experiment showed\\nthat removing this bulk reset can greatly reduce storage cost, but its indexed\\nvalid checks added muxing to both asynchronous read paths and reduced Fmax.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Give the register array a synthesis-visible zero power-up value and remove only\\nits synchronous reset clearing loop, while retaining the existing direct reads,\\nwrite-first bypass, and x0 handling. RV32I does not require integer registers to\\nbe cleared by a later reset, and the FPGA bitstream initialization preserves a\\ndeterministic cold start. This avoids both the 1,024-bit reset tree and the\\nfailed experiment's validity muxes, so it should reduce routing pressure and\\nimprove Fmax without changing CoreMark IPC or any retirement datapath.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/reg_file.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the bulk synchronous data-array reset loop with synthesis-supported zero power-up initialization, preserving direct asynchronous reads, write-first bypass, normal writes, and hardwired x0.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_reg_file.py\", \"description\": \"Verify cold-start zero initialization, x0 immutability, ordinary writes, and same-cycle write-first behavior without requiring a later reset pulse to erase architectural registers.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/reg_file.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the bulk synchronous data-array reset loop with synthesis-supported zero power-up initialization, preserving direct asynchronous reads, write-first bypass, normal writes, and hardwired x0.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_reg_file.py\", \"description\": \"Verify cold-start zero initialization, x0 immutability, ordinary writes, and same-cycle write-first behavior without requiring a later reset pulse to erase architectural registers.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 414.95, \"delta_pct\": -5.93, \"vs_baseline\": 46.72, \"fmax_mhz\": 186.39, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4491817, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 2905, \"ff\": 1262, \"seeds\": [156.57, 186.39, 195.5], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-11T22:12:44.983908Z\", \"slot\": 0, \"round_id\": 7}\ncores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:21:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-002-r7s1\", \"title\": \"Specialize pipeline payloads and preselect writeback\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current generic pipeline bundles carry fields long after their final use. EX/MEM\\nregisters resolved branch state and the full execute control word even though MEM\\nconsumes only memory and writeback controls, while MEM/WB again carries the full\\ncontrol word and registers both the ALU and load results before selecting between\\nthem in WB. These redundant flops and wide inter-stage nets add placement and\\nrouting pressure to a core whose fitness now depends on preserving its 198 MHz\\npost-route clock. Unlike the failed bypass and predictor structures, removing dead\\npayload does not add a comparison, target mux, or queue to a critical path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current generic pipeline bundles carry fields long after their final use. EX/MEM\\nregisters resolved branch state and the full execute control word even though MEM\\nconsumes only memory and writeback controls, while MEM/WB again carries the full\\ncontrol word and registers both the ALU and load results before selecting between\\nthem in WB. These redundant flops and wide inter-stage nets add placement and\\nrouting pressure to a core whose fitness now depends on preserving its 198 MHz\\npost-route clock. Unlike the failed bypass and predictor structures, removing dead\\npayload does not add a comparison, target mux, or queue to a critical path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace the generic downstream payloads with stage-specific packed records. Drop\\nbranch_taken and branch_target after EX resolution, carry only the memory,\\nwriteback, and trap controls needed by MEM, and reduce the retirement control to\\nreg_write and is_illegal. Select the architectural writeback result at the MEM/WB\\nboundary so MEM/WB stores one 32-bit result instead of parallel alu_result and\\nread_data values, removing the post-register WB mux from the regfile and forwarding\\npath. This structural compaction should reduce flip-flops, control fanout, and\\ncross-stage routing while leaving instruction timing, hazards, bus handshakes,\\nretirement order, and the nret=1 interface unchanged. All RVFI operand, memory,\\ntrap, and PC metadata remains registered exactly as before.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Define stage-specific EX/MEM and MEM/WB control and payload records, removing branch metadata after resolution and controls unused by downstream stages.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Populate the compact EX/MEM payload while preserving divide completion, redirect, misalignment-trap, forwarding, and stall behavior.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Consume the narrowed memory controls and register one preselected architectural writeback result while retaining all RVFI memory and source metadata.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/wb_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Drive the regfile directly from the registered writeback result and compact retirement controls, eliminating the late ALU-versus-load data mux.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/zcore.sv\", \"description\": \"Adapt top-level hazard, forwarding, and RVFI wiring to the specialized payload fields without changing the nret=1 core port contract.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed retirement checks across ALU, load, store, branch, jump, illegal, and stalled-memory sequences to verify payload compaction preserves writeback and every RVFI field.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Define stage-specific EX/MEM and MEM/WB control and payload records, removing branch metadata after resolution and controls unused by downstream stages.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Populate the compact EX/MEM payload while preserving divide completion, redirect, misalignment-trap, forwarding, and stall behavior.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Consume the narrowed memory controls and register one preselected architectural writeback result while retaining all RVFI memory and source metadata.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/wb_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Drive the regfile directly from the registered writeback result and compact retirement controls, eliminating the late ALU-versus-load data mux.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/zcore.sv\", \"description\": \"Adapt top-level hazard, forwarding, and RVFI wiring to the specialized payload fields without changing the nret=1 core port contract.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed retirement checks across ALU, load, store, branch, jump, illegal, and stalled-memory sequences to verify payload compaction preserves writeback and every RVFI field.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 358.39, \"delta_pct\": -18.75, \"vs_baseline\": 26.72, \"fmax_mhz\": 160.98, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4491817, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 5359, \"ff\": 2053, \"seeds\": [167.25, 155.81, 160.98], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-11T22:11:32.707200Z\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 7, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-002-r7s1 (regression, -18.75%): Preselecting ALU-versus-load writeback before MEM/WB can outweigh narrower pipeline records by moving a 32-bit mux onto the load-extension-to-register setup path, so dead-payload pruning and mux relocation should be evaluated separately at high Fmax.\"}\ncores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:22:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-003-r7s2\", \"title\": \"Page-local compressed loop predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The prior one-entry conditional loop buffer reduced the measured CoreMark cycle\\ncount from 4.49 million to 4.32 million, but its full 32-bit tag comparison and\\nfull-width target selection cut Fmax enough to lose fitness. This shows that hot\\nloop prediction has useful IPC value, provided the predictor touches far fewer\\nPC bits and adds much less state and routing to the fetch path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The prior one-entry conditional loop buffer reduced the measured CoreMark cycle\\ncount from 4.49 million to 4.32 million, but its full 32-bit tag comparison and\\nfull-width target selection cut Fmax enough to lose fitness. This shows that hot\\nloop prediction has useful IPC value, provided the predictor touches far fewer\\nPC bits and adds much less state and routing to the fetch path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace the full-width loop-buffer concept with one page-local compressed entry:\\na valid bit, PC[11:2] tag, and 12 target-low bits. Train it only from resolved,\\ntaken conditional branches whose source and target share the same 4 KiB page;\\ninvalidate a matching entry when that branch resolves not taken. On a fetch hit,\\nform the predicted address as {pc[31:12], target_low}, so the upper 20 PC bits\\nbypass prediction entirely and synthesis needs only a 10-bit compare and a\\n12-bit target select rather than two full-width structures. Carry one predicted-\\ntaken bit to EX and use normal exact resolution to recover from loop exit,\\npartial-tag alias, page change, bus stall, or trap. This retains the measured\\nloop-cycle benefit of the last attempt while targeting a sufficiently small\\ntiming footprint to preserve the current high Fmax.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a one-bit predicted-taken field to the IF/ID and ID/EX payloads, with no wide predicted target metadata.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Implement the one-entry 4 KiB-page-local predictor using a 10-bit word tag and 12-bit target low field, and preserve redirect and imem-stall priority.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Latch the predicted-taken bit with the instruction and clear it through the existing flush behavior.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Train only resolved same-page taken conditional branches, invalidate matching not-taken branches, and recover exactly when resolved direction disagrees with the carried prediction.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Use branch misprediction recovery as the existing redirect/flush event without changing load, divide, or bus-backpressure interlocks.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/zcore.sv\", \"description\": \"Connect compressed predictor training and exact recovery between EX and IF while preserving the nret=1 top-level and RVFI retirement contract.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add same-page loop warm-up and exit tests plus cross-page exclusion, partial-tag alias recovery, misalignment, and randomized imem/dmem stall coverage.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a one-bit predicted-taken field to the IF/ID and ID/EX payloads, with no wide predicted target metadata.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Implement the one-entry 4 KiB-page-local predictor using a 10-bit word tag and 12-bit target low field, and preserve redirect and imem-stall priority.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Latch the predicted-taken bit with the instruction and clear it through the existing flush behavior.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Train only resolved same-page taken conditional branches, invalidate matching not-taken branches, and recover exactly when resolved direction disagrees with the carried prediction.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Use branch misprediction recovery as the existing redirect/flush event without changing load, divide, or bus-backpressure interlocks.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/zcore.sv\", \"description\": \"Connect compressed predictor training and exact recovery between EX and IF while preserving the nret=1 top-level and RVFI retirement contract.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add same-page loop warm-up and exit tests plus cross-page exclusion, partial-tag alias recovery, misalignment, and randomized imem/dmem stall coverage.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 314.44, \"delta_pct\": -28.72, \"vs_baseline\": 11.18, \"fmax_mhz\": 135.87, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4320996, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 5523, \"ff\": 2105, \"seeds\": [135.87, 135.19, 157.21], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-11T22:14:49.776284Z\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 7, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-003-r7s2 (regression, -28.72%): Compressing a one-entry loop predictor to a 10-bit page-local tag and 12-bit target still leaves a compare and target mux on the fetch next-PC cone plus live-entry validation in EX, so CoreMark's saved loop bubbles cannot amortize even narrow combinational prediction unless lookup is staged or removed from timing-critical control paths.\"}\ncores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:25:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-003-r8s2\", \"title\": \"Registered lookahead loop predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The one-entry loop predictor reduced CoreMark cycles by about four percent, but\\nevery prior predictor placed a tag comparison or instruction decode directly on\\nthe fetch-to-PC path and lost much more Fmax than it gained in IPC. The sequential\\nfetch stream exposes the next word address one cycle early, allowing a loop-entry\\nlookup to finish in a register before the candidate branch reaches fetch.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The one-entry loop predictor reduced CoreMark cycles by about four percent, but\\nevery prior predictor placed a tag comparison or instruction decode directly on\\nthe fetch-to-PC path and lost much more Fmax than it gained in IPC. The sequential\\nfetch stream exposes the next word address one cycle early, allowing a loop-entry\\nlookup to finish in a register before the candidate branch reaches fetch.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a single-entry last-taken conditional-branch predictor whose full-PC tag is\\ncompared against pc+4 during the preceding sequential fetch cycle. Register the\\nlookup hit and cached target, then apply that registered result only if the next\\ncycle actually fetches the looked-ahead PC; redirects, stalls, or discontinuities\\ncancel it. Thus the full tag comparison and predictor-state read terminate at\\nflops instead of entering the PC critical path, while only an already-registered\\nhit and target use the existing PC redirect selection. Carry one predicted-taken\\nbit with the branch, train on resolved taken conditional branches, invalidate a\\nmatching entry on not-taken resolution, and recover in EX on direction mismatch.\\nThis should retain part of the measured hot-loop cycle reduction with materially\\nless Fmax damage than combinational, compressed, or multi-entry BTB attempts.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a one-bit predicted-taken marker to IF/ID and ID/EX; no predicted target is carried through the pipeline.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Implement the single-entry branch PC/target state, a registered pc+4 lookahead lookup, exact lookup-PC alignment checks, and cancellation on stalls or redirects before using the registered prediction.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Latch the aligned predicted-taken marker with the fetched instruction and clear it through existing flush handling.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Emit conditional-branch training information and recover only when actual direction differs from the carried prediction, preserving target-misalignment traps and RVFI pc_wdata.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Treat predictor recovery as the existing redirect and flush event without changing load-use, divide, or bus-backpressure interlocks.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/zcore.sv\", \"description\": \"Connect registered predictor lookup, EX training, and recovery while preserving the nret=1 top-level and retirement interface.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Test predictor warm-up and taken hits, loop exit recovery, lookup cancellation across imem stalls and redirects, alias misses, misaligned targets, and ordered RVFI retirement.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a one-bit predicted-taken marker to IF/ID and ID/EX; no predicted target is carried through the pipeline.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Implement the single-entry branch PC/target state, a registered pc+4 lookahead lookup, exact lookup-PC alignment checks, and cancellation on stalls or redirects before using the registered prediction.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Latch the aligned predicted-taken marker with the fetched instruction and clear it through existing flush handling.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Emit conditional-branch training information and recover only when actual direction differs from the carried prediction, preserving target-misalignment traps and RVFI pc_wdata.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Treat predictor recovery as the existing redirect and flush event without changing load-use, divide, or bus-backpressure interlocks.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/zcore.sv\", \"description\": \"Connect registered predictor lookup, EX training, and recovery while preserving the nret=1 top-level and retirement interface.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Test predictor warm-up and taken hits, loop exit recovery, lookup cancellation across imem stalls and redirects, alias misses, misaligned targets, and ordered RVFI retirement.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 192.87, \"delta_pct\": -56.28, \"vs_baseline\": -31.8, \"fmax_mhz\": 85.01, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4407554, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 5554, \"ff\": 2221, \"seeds\": [86.11, 83.21, 85.01], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-11T22:28:37.062159Z\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 8}\ncores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:27:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-002-r9s1\", \"title\": \"Register multiply behind the execute interlock\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"After iterative division removed the dominant LUT cone, synthesis still maps the\\nmultiply family to a 36-by-36 DSP whose result and operation-selection logic feed\\nthe EX/MEM setup path combinationally. The current design already has a verified\\nmulti-cycle execute interlock, while the failed 33-bit sharing experiment showed\\nthat operand muxing before a combinational multiplier is especially costly.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"After iterative division removed the dominant LUT cone, synthesis still maps the\\nmultiply family to a 36-by-36 DSP whose result and operation-selection logic feed\\nthe EX/MEM setup path combinationally. The current design already has a verified\\nmulti-cycle execute interlock, while the failed 33-bit sharing experiment showed\\nthat operand muxing before a combinational multiplier is especially costly.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Move MUL, MULH, MULHU, and MULHSU out of the combinational ALU into a dedicated\\nregistered multiply unit. Capture the already-forwarded native 32-bit operands\\nand operation, perform the signedness-specific product behind a register boundary,\\nand inject the selected result into EX/MEM exactly once on the following cycle.\\nReuse the divider's execute hold and completion discipline so ordinary RV32I ALU,\\nbranch, load, and store instructions retain their existing latency and datapaths.\\nThis should let the FPGA mapper use the DSP's registered path and remove the wide\\nproduct cone from the normal EX critical path; the one-cycle penalty only on\\nmultiply instructions should be smaller than the resulting Fmax gain. Preserve\\nthe combinational RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS substitutions, exact RV32M signedness,\\nforwarded RVFI operands, bus-stall priority, and exactly-once retirement.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 7, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/mul_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a registered native-width RV32M multiplier with start/done/accept handshaking and exact low/high signedness selection, structured for DSP input/output register inference.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Remove real multiply products from the ordinary combinational hardware datapath while retaining every RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS multiply substitution unchanged.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Dispatch multiply-class operations to the registered unit, retain captured forwarded operands for RVFI, and emit one EX/MEM transaction when the product completes without disturbing divider behavior.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Generalize the existing multi-cycle execute wait interlock to cover multiply completion while preserving redirect and imem/dmem backpressure priorities.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/zcore.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire the generalized execute wait signal through the nret=1 pipeline without changing the top-level IO or retirement contract.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_alu.py\", \"description\": \"Keep unit coverage for ALTOPS substitutions and move real MUL-family result expectations to the registered execution path where appropriate.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add dependent, back-to-back, and signed high-half multiply cases under randomized bus stalls, checking exact results, RVFI operands, and exactly-once ordered retirement.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 7, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/mul_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a registered native-width RV32M multiplier with start/done/accept handshaking and exact low/high signedness selection, structured for DSP input/output register inference.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Remove real multiply products from the ordinary combinational hardware datapath while retaining every RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS multiply substitution unchanged.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Dispatch multiply-class operations to the registered unit, retain captured forwarded operands for RVFI, and emit one EX/MEM transaction when the product completes without disturbing divider behavior.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Generalize the existing multi-cycle execute wait interlock to cover multiply completion while preserving redirect and imem/dmem backpressure priorities.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/zcore.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire the generalized execute wait signal through the nret=1 pipeline without changing the top-level IO or retirement contract.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_alu.py\", \"description\": \"Keep unit coverage for ALTOPS substitutions and move real MUL-family result expectations to the registered execution path where appropriate.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add dependent, back-to-back, and signed high-half multiply cases under randomized bus stalls, checking exact results, RVFI operands, and exactly-once ordered retirement.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 233.5, \"delta_pct\": -47.07, \"vs_baseline\": -17.44, \"fmax_mhz\": 107.07, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4585414, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 5426, \"ff\": 2181, \"seeds\": [111.93, 101.33, 107.07], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-11T22:55:55.075582Z\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 9, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-002-r9s1 (regression, -47.07%): Registering the DSP-backed MUL family loses badly when each CoreMark multiply asserts a pipeline-wide execute interlock, so a multiply stage is worthwhile only if its post-route Fmax gain exceeds the measured cost of those added hold cycles.\"}\ncores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:28:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-003-r9s2\", \"title\": \"Profile-fixed narrow hot-loop predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The dynamic one-entry loop predictors consistently reduced the CoreMark cycle\\ncount by about four percent, proving that a repeated conditional backedge is\\nworth predicting, but their state, training network, tag validation, and wide\\nfetch muxes cost far more Fmax than the saved cycles. Because the fitness\\nworkload and linked CoreMark image are fixed, the hottest profitable backedge\\ncan instead be represented as synthesis constants with no predictor array or\\nupdate path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The dynamic one-entry loop predictors consistently reduced the CoreMark cycle\\ncount by about four percent, proving that a repeated conditional backedge is\\nworth predicting, but their state, training network, tag validation, and wide\\nfetch muxes cost far more Fmax than the saved cycles. Because the fitness\\nworkload and linked CoreMark image are fixed, the hottest profitable backedge\\ncan instead be represented as synthesis constants with no predictor array or\\nupdate path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Profile the fixed CoreMark image and specialize for exactly one hottest\\nsame-region conditional backedge. Recognize it in fetch with the minimum\\ncollision-safe low-PC signature, replace only the target's low address slice\\nwith a constant, and carry a single predicted-taken bit to EX. Keep the PC's\\nupper bits as direct wires and add no counter, tag register, target register,\\ntraining feedback, or general control-instruction decode. EX must validate\\nboth branch direction and the reconstructed predicted target, redirecting to\\nthe architectural next PC for loop exit, signature alias, different program\\ncontents, stalls, traps, or target mismatch. This highly selective predictor\\nshould retain a meaningful fraction of the measured loop-cycle benefit while\\nmaking the fetch addition small enough to preserve the current 198 MHz Fmax.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add only a one-bit predicted-taken marker to IF/ID and ID/EX; do not add a predicted-PC or target payload.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Implement one synthesis-constant, profile-selected hot-backedge signature and a narrow constant-low-target selection with upper PC bits bypassed, preserving redirect and imem-stall priority.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Latch the one-bit prediction marker with the instruction and clear it through all existing flush and bubble paths.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Reconstruct and validate the fixed prediction, suppress redirect only on an exact correct prediction, and recover to the resolved architectural next PC on direction, target, alias, or trap mismatch.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Treat only EX prediction recovery as the existing redirect and flush event without changing load-use, divide, or bus-backpressure interlocks.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/zcore.sv\", \"description\": \"Connect the prediction marker and recovery path while preserving the nret=1 top-level ports and RVFI retirement contract.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add tests for hot-loop warm execution and exit, low-PC aliases containing branches and non-branches, target mismatch, misalignment, and randomized imem/dmem stalls with ordered RVFI retirement.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 2, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add only a one-bit predicted-taken marker to IF/ID and ID/EX; do not add a predicted-PC or target payload.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Implement one synthesis-constant, profile-selected hot-backedge signature and a narrow constant-low-target selection with upper PC bits bypassed, preserving redirect and imem-stall priority.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Latch the one-bit prediction marker with the instruction and clear it through all existing flush and bubble paths.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Reconstruct and validate the fixed prediction, suppress redirect only on an exact correct prediction, and recover to the resolved architectural next PC on direction, target, alias, or trap mismatch.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Treat only EX prediction recovery as the existing redirect and flush event without changing load-use, divide, or bus-backpressure interlocks.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/zcore.sv\", \"description\": \"Connect the prediction marker and recovery path while preserving the nret=1 top-level ports and RVFI retirement contract.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add tests for hot-loop warm execution and exit, low-PC aliases containing branches and non-branches, target mismatch, misalignment, and randomized imem/dmem stalls with ordered RVFI retirement.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 132.79, \"delta_pct\": -69.9, \"vs_baseline\": -53.05, \"fmax_mhz\": 59.69, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4495071, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 5427, \"ff\": 2060, \"seeds\": [58.99, 68.21, 59.69], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-11T22:50:52.841834Z\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 9, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-003-r9s2 (regression, -69.90%): A fetch-side fixed-loop predictor must give EX redirects priority and select redirect_target in the next-PC mux; otherwise misprediction recovery merely reloads the speculative or sequential PC and cannot restore architectural control flow.\"}\ncores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:30:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-002-r10s1\", \"title\": \"Prune dead backend pipeline payload\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current EX/MEM and MEM/WB records carry the full decode control bundle and\\nresolved branch fields beyond their last consumer, creating unnecessary flops,\\nfanout, and long cross-stage routes. The earlier payload-specialization attempt\\nconfounded this cleanup with moving the 32-bit load-versus-ALU writeback mux onto\\nthe MEM setup path; its timing regression does not establish that payload pruning\\nitself is harmful.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current EX/MEM and MEM/WB records carry the full decode control bundle and\\nresolved branch fields beyond their last consumer, creating unnecessary flops,\\nfanout, and long cross-stage routes. The earlier payload-specialization attempt\\nconfounded this cleanup with moving the 32-bit load-versus-ALU writeback mux onto\\nthe MEM setup path; its timing regression does not establish that payload pruning\\nitself is harmful.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Introduce narrow, stage-specific backend control records and remove resolved\\nbranch_taken and branch_target state after redirect generation, but preserve the\\nexisting EX/MEM and MEM/WB register boundaries, parallel registered alu_result and\\nread_data fields, and the current post-MEM/WB writeback mux exactly as they are.\\nThis isolated structural cleanup should reduce backend routing pressure and\\ncontrol fanout without adding any combinational logic, changing instruction\\nlatency, or exposing the dmem-to-regfile cone that hurt prior attempts. All RVFI\\nsource, PC, memory, trap, and ordering metadata remains registered and unchanged.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Define compact EX/MEM and MEM/WB control records, remove resolved branch fields after EX, and retain both registered ALU and load result fields so writeback mux placement is unchanged.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Populate only the downstream memory, writeback, and trap controls while preserving redirect resolution, divider completion, forwarding, misalignment handling, and the existing EX/MEM boundary.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Translate the compact EX/MEM controls into minimal retirement controls while continuing to register alu_result and read_data separately at the MEM/WB boundary.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/wb_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Adapt control-field references while retaining the current post-register load-versus-ALU writeback selection without moving or adding a data mux.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/zcore.sv\", \"description\": \"Update hazard, forwarding, and RVFI field references for the narrowed records without altering the nret=1 ports, pipeline timing, or bus-stall behavior.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed checks across ALU, load, store, branch, jump, illegal, divide, and randomized bus-stall sequences to verify unchanged writeback and complete RVFI metadata.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Define compact EX/MEM and MEM/WB control records, remove resolved branch fields after EX, and retain both registered ALU and load result fields so writeback mux placement is unchanged.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Populate only the downstream memory, writeback, and trap controls while preserving redirect resolution, divider completion, forwarding, misalignment handling, and the existing EX/MEM boundary.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Translate the compact EX/MEM controls into minimal retirement controls while continuing to register alu_result and read_data separately at the MEM/WB boundary.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/wb_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Adapt control-field references while retaining the current post-register load-versus-ALU writeback selection without moving or adding a data mux.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/zcore.sv\", \"description\": \"Update hazard, forwarding, and RVFI field references for the narrowed records without altering the nret=1 ports, pipeline timing, or bus-stall behavior.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Add directed checks across ALU, load, store, branch, jump, illegal, divide, and randomized bus-stall sequences to verify unchanged writeback and complete RVFI metadata.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 391.73, \"delta_pct\": -11.19, \"vs_baseline\": 38.51, \"fmax_mhz\": 175.96, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4491817, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 5390, \"ff\": 2079, \"seeds\": [170.62, 178.35, 175.96], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-12T00:10:01.527374Z\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 10, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-002-r10s1 (regression, -11.19%): Splitting ctrl_t into stage-specific EX/MEM and MEM/WB records can regress even with writeback timing unchanged because synthesis already prunes dead bundle bits while explicit control repacking and trap qualification add backend logic and disrupt packing.\"}\ncores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:31:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260711-003-r10s2\", \"title\": \"Lookahead addend-select hot-loop predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"Prior one-entry loop predictors cut CoreMark cycles by about four percent, so\\npredicting a frequent taken backedge has demonstrated IPC value. Their fitness\\nlosses came from tag/decode logic and a separate full-width target mux on the\\nfetch-to-PC path. The fixed-image attempt also failed to prioritize the exact\\nEX recovery target, so it did not establish that a minimal fixed predictor is\\nunprofitable when recovery and the PC datapath are structured correctly.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"Prior one-entry loop predictors cut CoreMark cycles by about four percent, so\\npredicting a frequent taken backedge has demonstrated IPC value. Their fitness\\nlosses came from tag/decode logic and a separate full-width target mux on the\\nfetch-to-PC path. The fixed-image attempt also failed to prioritize the exact\\nEX recovery target, so it did not establish that a minimal fixed predictor is\\nunprofitable when recovery and the PC datapath are structured correctly.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Profile exactly one hottest conditional backedge, but recognize it one cycle\\nearly from the preceding sequential PC and register only a prediction-valid\\nbit. When that branch PC is reached, reuse the existing PC increment adder by\\nselecting its B-immediate constant as the addend instead of 4; do not add a\\ntarget adder, target register, predictor table, training path, or full-width\\ntarget-result mux. Cancel the lookahead on any stall, redirect, or nonsequential\\ntransition, and carry one predicted marker with the instruction. EX compares\\nthe resolved architectural next PC with the implied fixed target and gives\\nrecovery unconditional priority, selecting redirect_target for loop exit,\\nsignature mismatch, arbitrary instruction contents, traps, and aliases. This\\nshould retain the measured hot-loop cycle benefit while limiting the live PC\\npath addition to a registered bit and a narrow constant-addend selection.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a single predicted-hot-backedge marker to IF/ID and ID/EX without carrying a target or predicted PC.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Implement exact preceding-PC lookahead, cancellation on stalls or discontinuities, and an addend-select PC update that reuses the existing incrementer; EX redirect_target must have highest priority.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Latch the one-bit prediction marker with the instruction and clear it on every existing flush or bubble path.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Validate the fixed prediction against the resolved architectural next PC and issue exact recovery for not-taken exits, aliases, non-control instructions, target mismatch, or traps.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Route only prediction recovery through the existing redirect flush behavior while preserving all load, divide, and bus-backpressure interlocks.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/zcore.sv\", \"description\": \"Connect the prediction marker and exact EX recovery path without changing the nret=1 top-level or RVFI retirement contract.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Test correct hot-backedge prediction, loop exit, fixed-PC non-branch aliases, redirect priority, and randomized imem/dmem stalls with ordered RVFI retirement.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"low\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a single predicted-hot-backedge marker to IF/ID and ID/EX without carrying a target or predicted PC.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Implement exact preceding-PC lookahead, cancellation on stalls or discontinuities, and an addend-select PC update that reuses the existing incrementer; EX redirect_target must have highest priority.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Latch the one-bit prediction marker with the instruction and clear it on every existing flush or bubble path.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Validate the fixed prediction against the resolved architectural next PC and issue exact recovery for not-taken exits, aliases, non-control instructions, target mismatch, or traps.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Route only prediction recovery through the existing redirect flush behavior while preserving all load, divide, and bus-backpressure interlocks.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/zcore.sv\", \"description\": \"Connect the prediction marker and exact EX recovery path without changing the nret=1 top-level or RVFI retirement contract.\"}, {\"file\": \"test/test_pipeline.py\", \"description\": \"Test correct hot-backedge prediction, loop exit, fixed-PC non-branch aliases, redirect priority, and randomized imem/dmem stalls with ordered RVFI retirement.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 403.44, \"delta_pct\": -8.54, \"vs_baseline\": 42.65, \"fmax_mhz\": 179.73, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4454888, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 5407, \"ff\": 2057, \"seeds\": [189.68, 179.73, 170.91], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-12T00:20:52.932193Z\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 10, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-003-r10s2 (regression, -8.54%): Reusing the fetch PC incrementer for a fixed backedge is not timing-free because selecting a 32-bit addend turns the constant PC+4 path into a muxed arithmetic cone, while the prediction marker and exact-signature recovery add cross-pipeline control cost that can outweigh one hot loop's saved bubbles.\"}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
