{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,240p' cores/bench/LESSONS.md && jq -c 'select(.hypothesis.category == \\\"predictor\\\" or .category == \\\"predictor\\\") | {id, outcome, fitness, hypothesis, implementation_notes}' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | tail -20 && sed -n '1,220p' schemas/hypothesis.schema.json\"","aggregated_output":"- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-002-r1s1 (improvement, +55.97%): Replacing the combinational DIV/REM cone with a shared radix-2 iterative M-unit greatly improves Fmax and routing efficiency when CoreMark's low dynamic divide frequency amortizes the resulting pipeline freezes.\n- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-001-r2s0 (broken, n/a): Opcode-qualified load-use interlocks must also qualify or zero RVFI unused-source metadata, or raw rs1/rs2 fields can expose stale pre-load values that reg_ch0 rejects even when the instruction does not architecturally consume those operands.\n- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-003-r2s2 (regression, -22.31%): Exact JAL prediction loses overall when J-immediate reconstruction and a 32-bit target adder sit directly on the fetch-to-PC critical path, so direct-target prediction must be staged or kept off the Fmax-limiting next-PC cone.\n- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-002-r3s1 (regression, -37.74%): A zero-latency two-entry fetch skid buffer can lose badly when its queue-head/live-response mux and count/valid control remain on the combinational IF-to-ID path, so limited prefetch during backend holds must amortize the front-end timing and routing cost.\n- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-003-r3s2 (regression, -44.80%): Caching resolved targets does not make prediction timing-free: an asynchronous direct-mapped micro-BTB's tag compare, counter/unconditional decision, and 32-bit target mux on the fetch next-PC cone can overwhelm CoreMark's saved control bubbles, so BTB lookup must be staged or mapped to a timing-friendly memory structure.\n- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-001-r4s0 (regression, -19.79%): Replacing a reset register array with per-entry validity bits can hurt an asynchronous 2R1W register file because indexed valid checks add muxing to both combinational read paths without making its write-first multi-read structure memory-inference-friendly.\n- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-002-r4s1 (regression, -22.95%): Sharing RV32M variants through an opcode-selected signed 33x33 product can hurt FPGA timing because widening the native 32-bit multiply and placing sign/zero-extension muxes before it outweighs eliminating parallel products; prefer native-width multiplication with post-product sign correction.\n- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-003-r4s2 (regression, -48.43%): Registering a micro-BTB lookup is insufficient when the four-entry tag/target/counter flop arrays and widened IF/ID prediction payload add enough placement and routing pressure to outweigh saving one CoreMark taken-control bubble; a staged predictor must also use compact, memory-friendly state and metadata.\n- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-003-r5s2 (regression, -59.29%): An adder-free RAS is not a timing-free return predictor when canonical-JALR decode and a 32-bit registered-stack-top mux still drive the fetch next-PC cone, so CoreMark return bubbles cannot amortize the added stack state and cross-pipeline control routing.\n- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-002-r6s1 (regression, -45.75%): Removing a load-to-store-data bubble can lose badly when the late WB-to-MEM bypass adds a register compare and 32-bit mux directly to store-data/RVFI routing, so even non-ALU forwarding must be kept off timing-sensitive cross-stage paths.\n- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-002-r7s1 (regression, -18.75%): Preselecting ALU-versus-load writeback before MEM/WB can outweigh narrower pipeline records by moving a 32-bit mux onto the load-extension-to-register setup path, so dead-payload pruning and mux relocation should be evaluated separately at high Fmax.\n- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-003-r7s2 (regression, -28.72%): Compressing a one-entry loop predictor to a 10-bit page-local tag and 12-bit target still leaves a compare and target mux on the fetch next-PC cone plus live-entry validation in EX, so CoreMark's saved loop bubbles cannot amortize even narrow combinational prediction unless lookup is staged or removed from timing-critical control paths.\n- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-002-r8s1 (regression, -17.54%): Collapsing MEM/WB into EX/MEM removes flops but also removes the timing boundary before load formatting, writeback selection, regfile commit, and RVFI fanout, so retirement-stage state can be cheaper than exposing the full dmem-to-commit cone in one cycle.\n- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-002-r9s1 (regression, -47.07%): Registering the DSP-backed MUL family loses badly when each CoreMark multiply asserts a pipeline-wide execute interlock, so a multiply stage is worthwhile only if its post-route Fmax gain exceeds the measured cost of those added hold cycles.\n- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-003-r9s2 (regression, -69.90%): A fetch-side fixed-loop predictor must give EX redirects priority and select redirect_target in the next-PC mux; otherwise misprediction recovery merely reloads the speculative or sequential PC and cannot restore architectural control flow.\n- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-001-r10s0 (regression, -45.03%): Making only cold MULH/MULHU/MULHSU iterative still loses when the new shift-add state, completion/result muxing, and pipeline-wide execute interlock add always-present routing cost, so rare dynamic use does not make a separate multi-cycle unit timing-free.\n- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-002-r10s1 (regression, -11.19%): Splitting ctrl_t into stage-specific EX/MEM and MEM/WB records can regress even with writeback timing unchanged because synthesis already prunes dead bundle bits while explicit control repacking and trap qualification add backend logic and disrupt packing.\n- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-003-r10s2 (regression, -8.54%): Reusing the fetch PC incrementer for a fixed backedge is not timing-free because selecting a 32-bit addend turns the constant PC+4 path into a muxed arithmetic cone, while the prediction marker and exact-signature recovery add cross-pipeline control cost that can outweigh one hot loop's saved bubbles.\n- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-001-r11s0 (regression, -13.40%): Deriving MULHSU from the signed product can lose despite removing a 64-bit multiplier because the conditional 32-bit correction adder extends the product-to-ALU-output cone, so multiplier-count reduction alone is not a reliable timing optimization.\n- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-002-r11s1 (regression, -19.63%): Fully registering a two-entry fetch queue head avoids the live imem-response mux but can still lose when the added front-end latency and taken-redirect recovery bubbles outweigh instruction-stall overlap, so fetch buffering must preserve the common-case fetch-to-decode latency as well as isolate timing.\n- 2026-07-11 hyp-20260711-003-r11s2 (regression, -35.66%): Resolved-target folding can still destroy Fmax without a fetch-side lookup because exact source-PC/instruction matching plus `fold_inject` creates an EX-to-ID/EX replay cone and selects target+4 on the redirect path, so branch folding needs a registered replay boundary rather than same-cycle decode injection.\n{\"id\":\"hyp-20260711-003-r1s2\",\"outcome\":\"broken\",\"fitness\":null,\"hypothesis\":{\"motivation\":\"The current pipeline waits until EX to redirect every taken branch and jump,\\nflushing younger work even for regular loop backedges and direct calls. CoreMark\\ncontains frequent counted loops, so these repeated control penalties directly\\nreduce iterations per cycle under the benchmark's instruction-bus stalls.\\n\",\"hypothesis\":\"Add a minimal static predictor in IF that always predicts direct JAL targets and\\npredicts conditional branches backward-taken, forward-not-taken. Carry the\\npredicted next PC with each instruction and make EX redirect and flush only when\\nthe resolved next PC differs from that prediction. This should eliminate most\\nloop-backedge and direct-jump bubbles with little state or LUT cost, preserving\\nFmax better than a tagged BTB or dynamic history table.\\n\",\"expected_impact\":{\"fitness_delta_pct\":9,\"confidence\":\"medium\"},\"changes\":[{\"file\":\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\",\"description\":\"Add predicted-next-PC metadata to the IF/ID and ID/EX pipeline payloads.\"},{\"file\":\"rtl/if_stage.sv\",\"description\":\"Decode fetch-time branch/JAL immediates, apply backward-taken and direct-JAL prediction, and emit the selected predicted next PC.\"},{\"file\":\"rtl/id_stage.sv\",\"description\":\"Pipeline the fetch prediction metadata alongside the instruction into EX.\"},{\"file\":\"rtl/ex_stage.sv\",\"description\":\"Compare resolved next PC with the carried prediction and request recovery only on a mismatch, while retaining architectural pc_wdata and trap behavior.\"},{\"file\":\"rtl/core.sv\",\"description\":\"Rename and connect the EX recovery signals through IF and hazard control as a misprediction redirect.\"},{\"file\":\"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\",\"description\":\"Treat the EX recovery indication as a misprediction flush so correctly predicted taken control flow is not squashed.\"},{\"file\":\"test/test_pipeline.py\",\"description\":\"Add directed tests for correct backward-branch and JAL predictions, forward-branch fall-through, and taken/not-taken misprediction recovery.\"}]},\"implementation_notes\":null}\n{\"id\":\"hyp-20260711-003-r2s2\",\"outcome\":\"regression\",\"fitness\":342.69,\"hypothesis\":{\"motivation\":\"The pipeline currently treats every JAL as an EX-stage redirect, discarding the\\nalready fetched target instruction and paying a control bubble for direct calls\\nand unconditional jumps. A JAL target is encoded entirely in the fetched\\ninstruction, so it can be predicted exactly without a BTB, history table, or\\naliasing risk. The previous combined JAL and conditional-branch proposal failed\\nduring implementation compilation, making a narrower predictor the best path to\\nretain useful control-flow speedup with much lower RTL and timing risk.\\n\",\"hypothesis\":\"Decode only the JAL opcode and J-immediate in IF, select pc+imm as the predicted\\nnext fetch address, and carry that predicted next PC to EX. EX will compare the\\narchitecturally resolved next PC against the prediction and redirect/flush only\\non a mismatch, including recovery to pc+4 for a misaligned JAL trap. All other\\ninstructions remain predict-not-taken and preserve the existing recovery path.\\nThis stateless, always-correct prediction should remove the EX redirect penalty\\nfor every aligned direct call and jump while adding only a small immediate adder\\nand metadata register, so iterations per cycle should rise with little Fmax loss.\\n\",\"expected_impact\":{\"fitness_delta_pct\":4,\"confidence\":\"medium\"},\"changes\":[{\"file\":\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\",\"description\":\"Add predicted-next-PC metadata to the IF/ID and ID/EX payloads.\"},{\"file\":\"rtl/if_stage.sv\",\"description\":\"Recognize fetched JAL instructions, form their J-immediate target, advance the PC to that target, and emit the selected predicted next PC while respecting stalls and recovery priority.\"},{\"file\":\"rtl/id_stage.sv\",\"description\":\"Latch predicted-next-PC metadata with the instruction into ID/EX.\"},{\"file\":\"rtl/ex_stage.sv\",\"description\":\"Generate a recovery redirect only when resolved next PC differs from the carried prediction, including misaligned-JAL trap recovery, while keeping architectural RVFI pc_wdata unchanged.\"},{\"file\":\"rtl/core.sv\",\"description\":\"Wire the EX recovery indication and target through the existing IF and hazard redirect paths without changing the nret=1 interface.\"},{\"file\":\"test/test_pipeline.py\",\"description\":\"Add directed checks that aligned JAL reaches its target without a recovery bubble and that misaligned JAL traps, suppresses rd writeback, and recovers to pc+4 under bus stalls.\"}]},\"implementation_notes\":\"\"}\n{\"id\":\"hyp-20260711-003-r3s2\",\"outcome\":\"regression\",\"fitness\":243.48,\"hypothesis\":{\"motivation\":\"CoreMark repeatedly executes taken loop branches, calls, returns, and other jumps,\\nbut the current core waits until EX to redirect every one and discards younger\\nwork. The prior exact-JAL predictor proved that putting immediate reconstruction\\nand a 32-bit target adder in the fetch next-PC cone costs more Fmax than it saves\\nin control bubbles. A learned target cache can recover the same control-flow\\nopportunities without recomputing a target in fetch.\\n\",\"hypothesis\":\"Add a small direct-mapped micro-BTB trained by resolved control instructions in\\nEX. Each entry holds a valid bit, a PC tag, the already-computed target, an\\nunconditional flag, and a 2-bit saturating direction counter for conditional\\nbranches. Fetch predicts only on a valid tag hit and selects the cached target;\\notherwise it remains sequential. Carry the predicted next PC with the instruction\\nand make EX flush and redirect only when the architectural next PC differs. This\\nshould remove repeated loop, JAL, JALR, and return penalties while keeping J/B\\nimmediate decoding and target addition entirely off the fetch critical path.\\nA modest table with a shallow low-PC index should limit LUT, fanout, and routing\\ncost; tags prevent destructive alias predictions, and normal EX recovery preserves\\ncorrectness under cold misses, direction changes, replacement, and bus stalls.\\n\",\"expected_impact\":{\"fitness_delta_pct\":5,\"confidence\":\"medium\"},\"changes\":[{\"file\":\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\",\"description\":\"Add predicted-next-PC metadata to the IF/ID and ID/EX payloads so each control instruction can be checked against the exact fetch decision.\"},{\"file\":\"rtl/if_stage.sv\",\"description\":\"Add a small direct-mapped tagged BTB with cached targets, unconditional classification, and 2-bit branch counters; predict from a tag hit without a fetch-time target adder and update entries from EX resolution.\"},{\"file\":\"rtl/id_stage.sv\",\"description\":\"Pipeline the predicted next PC alongside the instruction while preserving existing flush and stall behavior.\"},{\"file\":\"rtl/ex_stage.sv\",\"description\":\"Export resolved control-flow training information and generate recovery only when the resolved architectural next PC differs from the carried prediction, including not-taken and misalignment recovery.\"},{\"file\":\"rtl/zcore.sv\",\"description\":\"Connect BTB training and misprediction recovery between EX and IF without changing the nret=1 top-level interface or RVFI retirement path.\"},{\"file\":\"test/test_pipeline.py\",\"description\":\"Add directed repeated-loop, JAL, JALR-return, direction-change, alias-replacement, misalignment, and randomized bus-stall tests checking prediction recovery and ordered RVFI behavior.\"}]},\"implementation_notes\":\"\"}\n{\"id\":\"hyp-20260711-003-r4s2\",\"outcome\":\"regression\",\"fitness\":227.47,\"hypothesis\":{\"motivation\":\"CoreMark repeatedly executes taken loop branches and jumps, so resolving every\\ncontrol transfer in EX wastes front-end cycles. The two working predictor\\nattempts regressed because target generation or an asynchronous BTB lookup fed\\nthe fetch PC in the same cycle, collapsing FPGA Fmax despite modest IPC gains.\\nThe prior results indicate that predictor timing, rather than prediction value,\\nis the limiting problem.\\n\",\"hypothesis\":\"Add a very small tagged BTB whose lookup result is registered before it can\\nredirect fetch. On cycle N the current PC indexes the table while fetch proceeds\\nsequentially; at the clock edge the hit, cached target, direction decision, and\\nlookup PC are captured. On cycle N+1 a valid registered prediction redirects to\\nthe cached target and squashes only the single sequential instruction fetched\\nbehind it. EX trains the table and still performs exact recovery whenever the\\nresolved architectural next PC differs from the prediction carried with the\\ncontrol instruction. This deliberately one-cycle-late predictor saves one of\\nthe existing taken-control bubbles while keeping tag comparison, counter decode,\\ntarget storage, and immediate arithmetic off the same-cycle PC next-state cone.\\nA four-entry table and registered prediction outputs should preserve most of the\\ncurrent 198 MHz placement quality, making a smaller IPC gain worthwhile on the\\nfitness product.\\n\",\"expected_impact\":{\"fitness_delta_pct\":3,\"confidence\":\"medium\"},\"changes\":[{\"file\":\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\",\"description\":\"Carry the fetch prediction identity and predicted next PC through IF/ID and ID/EX so EX can validate the delayed decision precisely.\"},{\"file\":\"rtl/if_stage.sv\",\"description\":\"Implement a four-entry tagged BTB with registered lookup outputs, one-cycle-late target redirect, single-instruction squash, EX training, and redirect/stall priority that never places table lookup logic on the same-cycle PC cone.\"},{\"file\":\"rtl/id_stage.sv\",\"description\":\"Latch delayed-prediction metadata with the associated control instruction and preserve it across pipeline holds.\"},{\"file\":\"rtl/ex_stage.sv\",\"description\":\"Export BTB training data and recover only when the resolved next PC disagrees with the prediction associated with the instruction, including not-taken and misalignment cases.\"},{\"file\":\"rtl/zcore.sv\",\"description\":\"Connect registered BTB prediction, EX training, and misprediction recovery while retaining the nret=1 top-level and ordered RVFI path.\"},{\"file\":\"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\",\"description\":\"Distinguish the delayed predictor's one-instruction squash from an EX misprediction flush and preserve existing bus and divide hold priorities.\"},{\"file\":\"test/test_pipeline.py\",\"description\":\"Add repeated-loop, jump, direction-change, alias-replacement, and simultaneous bus-stall tests that verify the delayed prediction saves one bubble and always recovers with exact ordered RVFI retirement.\"}]},\"implementation_notes\":\"\"}\n{\"id\":\"hyp-20260711-003-r5s2\",\"outcome\":\"regression\",\"fitness\":179.59,\"hypothesis\":{\"motivation\":\"CoreMark contains many function returns, and the current pipeline pays the full\\nEX-stage redirect penalty for every JALR. Prior predictors reduced control-flow\\ncycles but lost much more Fmax to fetch-time target adders, PC-indexed tag arrays,\\ncounters, and wide prediction metadata. Canonical returns offer a narrower target:\\ntheir destination was already computed as the link address of an earlier call.\\n\",\"hypothesis\":\"Add a small return-address stack trained only by resolved calls and returns, and\\npredict only canonical return encodings such as JALR x0, 0(x1) or x5. Fetch selects\\nthe already-registered stack top, requiring no immediate reconstruction, target\\nadder, PC tag comparison, direction counter, or indexed target array. Carry only a\\none-bit predicted-return marker into EX; keep the stack top stable until that return\\nresolves, compare it with the architectural JALR target, and recover normally on a\\nmismatch, underflow, overflow alias, modified link register, or misalignment. A\\nshallow stack should remove a useful class of repeated indirect-control bubbles\\nwhile avoiding the timing and routing structures that caused every prior predictor\\nregression.\\n\",\"expected_impact\":{\"fitness_delta_pct\":3,\"confidence\":\"medium\"},\"changes\":[{\"file\":\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\",\"description\":\"Add a single predicted-return bit to the IF/ID and ID/EX payloads without carrying tags or 32-bit predicted targets through the pipeline.\"},{\"file\":\"rtl/if_stage.sv\",\"description\":\"Implement a shallow return-address stack, recognize only canonical return encodings, select its registered top as the predicted PC, and preserve redirect and bus-stall priority.\"},{\"file\":\"rtl/id_stage.sv\",\"description\":\"Pipeline the one-bit return-prediction marker with the associated instruction and clear it on existing flush paths.\"},{\"file\":\"rtl/ex_stage.sv\",\"description\":\"Classify resolved calls and returns for stack updates, suppress the normal JALR redirect only when a marked return matches the current stack top, and issue exact recovery on mismatch or misalignment.\"},{\"file\":\"rtl/zcore.sv\",\"description\":\"Connect return-stack training, stack-top validation, and recovery between EX and IF while preserving the nret=1 interface and RVFI retirement behavior.\"},{\"file\":\"test/test_pipeline.py\",\"description\":\"Add nested-call, canonical-return, link-register-modification, underflow, overflow, misalignment, and randomized bus-stall tests for prediction and exact recovery.\"}]},\"implementation_notes\":\"\"}\n{\"id\":\"hyp-20260711-003-r6s2\",\"outcome\":\"regression\",\"fitness\":360.87,\"hypothesis\":{\"motivation\":\"The baseline pays a redirect bubble for every taken conditional loop branch, but\\nall broader predictor attempts lost far more Fmax than their IPC gain because\\nthey added target arrays, counters, multi-entry selection, wide metadata, or\\nJALR/RAS routing. CoreMark's hot counted loops offer a much narrower opportunity:\\nthe same direct conditional branch and fixed target repeat many times before the\\nloop exit.\\n\",\"hypothesis\":\"Add a single-entry last-taken conditional-branch target buffer containing only a\\nvalid bit, the full branch PC, and its resolved target. A full-PC hit predicts the\\nbranch taken; a resolved taken conditional branch installs the entry, while a\\nresolved not-taken instance invalidates a matching entry. Restricting prediction\\nto direct conditional branches makes the cached target immutable for that PC, so\\nonly a one-bit predicted-taken marker must travel to EX. EX recovers on a cold\\ntaken branch or a predicted-taken loop exit and otherwise suppresses the existing\\nredirect. This should capture repeated loop backedges after one warm-up miss with\\nmuch less timing and placement cost than the failed multi-entry BTBs: there is no\\nindexed array, direction counter, unconditional-control decode, JALR path, or\\n32-bit predicted-next-PC pipeline payload.\\n\",\"expected_impact\":{\"fitness_delta_pct\":2,\"confidence\":\"low\"},\"changes\":[{\"file\":\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\",\"description\":\"Add only a predicted-taken bit to the IF/ID and ID/EX payloads, avoiding a wide predicted-target metadata path.\"},{\"file\":\"rtl/if_stage.sv\",\"description\":\"Implement the one-entry full-tag conditional loop target buffer, predict only on exact hits, and update or invalidate it from resolved conditional branches with recovery and bus-stall priority.\"},{\"file\":\"rtl/id_stage.sv\",\"description\":\"Latch the one-bit prediction marker with its instruction and clear it on existing flush paths.\"},{\"file\":\"rtl/ex_stage.sv\",\"description\":\"Train the entry from resolved conditional branches and generate recovery only when actual direction disagrees with the carried prediction, preserving misalignment traps and architectural pc_wdata.\"},{\"file\":\"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\",\"description\":\"Treat EX branch-prediction recovery as the existing redirect flush while preserving load, divide, and memory-backpressure priorities.\"},{\"file\":\"rtl/zcore.sv\",\"description\":\"Connect conditional-branch training and misprediction recovery between EX and IF without changing the nret=1 top-level or RVFI retirement path.\"},{\"file\":\"test/test_pipeline.py\",\"description\":\"Add repeated-loop warm-up, taken-hit, loop-exit invalidation, tag-miss, misaligned-target, and randomized bus-stall tests with exact ordered RVFI checks.\"}]},\"implementation_notes\":\"\"}\n{\"id\":\"hyp-20260711-003-r7s2\",\"outcome\":\"regression\",\"fitness\":314.44,\"hypothesis\":{\"motivation\":\"The prior one-entry conditional loop buffer reduced the measured CoreMark cycle\\ncount from 4.49 million to 4.32 million, but its full 32-bit tag comparison and\\nfull-width target selection cut Fmax enough to lose fitness. This shows that hot\\nloop prediction has useful IPC value, provided the predictor touches far fewer\\nPC bits and adds much less state and routing to the fetch path.\\n\",\"hypothesis\":\"Replace the full-width loop-buffer concept with one page-local compressed entry:\\na valid bit, PC[11:2] tag, and 12 target-low bits. Train it only from resolved,\\ntaken conditional branches whose source and target share the same 4 KiB page;\\ninvalidate a matching entry when that branch resolves not taken. On a fetch hit,\\nform the predicted address as {pc[31:12], target_low}, so the upper 20 PC bits\\nbypass prediction entirely and synthesis needs only a 10-bit compare and a\\n12-bit target select rather than two full-width structures. Carry one predicted-\\ntaken bit to EX and use normal exact resolution to recover from loop exit,\\npartial-tag alias, page change, bus stall, or trap. This retains the measured\\nloop-cycle benefit of the last attempt while targeting a sufficiently small\\ntiming footprint to preserve the current high Fmax.\\n\",\"expected_impact\":{\"fitness_delta_pct\":3,\"confidence\":\"low\"},\"changes\":[{\"file\":\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\",\"description\":\"Add a one-bit predicted-taken field to the IF/ID and ID/EX payloads, with no wide predicted target metadata.\"},{\"file\":\"rtl/if_stage.sv\",\"description\":\"Implement the one-entry 4 KiB-page-local predictor using a 10-bit word tag and 12-bit target low field, and preserve redirect and imem-stall priority.\"},{\"file\":\"rtl/id_stage.sv\",\"description\":\"Latch the predicted-taken bit with the instruction and clear it through the existing flush behavior.\"},{\"file\":\"rtl/ex_stage.sv\",\"description\":\"Train only resolved same-page taken conditional branches, invalidate matching not-taken branches, and recover exactly when resolved direction disagrees with the carried prediction.\"},{\"file\":\"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\",\"description\":\"Use branch misprediction recovery as the existing redirect/flush event without changing load, divide, or bus-backpressure interlocks.\"},{\"file\":\"rtl/zcore.sv\",\"description\":\"Connect compressed predictor training and exact recovery between EX and IF while preserving the nret=1 top-level and RVFI retirement contract.\"},{\"file\":\"test/test_pipeline.py\",\"description\":\"Add same-page loop warm-up and exit tests plus cross-page exclusion, partial-tag alias recovery, misalignment, and randomized imem/dmem stall coverage.\"}]},\"implementation_notes\":\"\"}\n{\"id\":\"hyp-20260711-003-r8s2\",\"outcome\":\"regression\",\"fitness\":192.87,\"hypothesis\":{\"motivation\":\"The one-entry loop predictor reduced CoreMark cycles by about four percent, but\\nevery prior predictor placed a tag comparison or instruction decode directly on\\nthe fetch-to-PC path and lost much more Fmax than it gained in IPC. The sequential\\nfetch stream exposes the next word address one cycle early, allowing a loop-entry\\nlookup to finish in a register before the candidate branch reaches fetch.\\n\",\"hypothesis\":\"Add a single-entry last-taken conditional-branch predictor whose full-PC tag is\\ncompared against pc+4 during the preceding sequential fetch cycle. Register the\\nlookup hit and cached target, then apply that registered result only if the next\\ncycle actually fetches the looked-ahead PC; redirects, stalls, or discontinuities\\ncancel it. Thus the full tag comparison and predictor-state read terminate at\\nflops instead of entering the PC critical path, while only an already-registered\\nhit and target use the existing PC redirect selection. Carry one predicted-taken\\nbit with the branch, train on resolved taken conditional branches, invalidate a\\nmatching entry on not-taken resolution, and recover in EX on direction mismatch.\\nThis should retain part of the measured hot-loop cycle reduction with materially\\nless Fmax damage than combinational, compressed, or multi-entry BTB attempts.\\n\",\"expected_impact\":{\"fitness_delta_pct\":2,\"confidence\":\"low\"},\"changes\":[{\"file\":\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\",\"description\":\"Add a one-bit predicted-taken marker to IF/ID and ID/EX; no predicted target is carried through the pipeline.\"},{\"file\":\"rtl/if_stage.sv\",\"description\":\"Implement the single-entry branch PC/target state, a registered pc+4 lookahead lookup, exact lookup-PC alignment checks, and cancellation on stalls or redirects before using the registered prediction.\"},{\"file\":\"rtl/id_stage.sv\",\"description\":\"Latch the aligned predicted-taken marker with the fetched instruction and clear it through existing flush handling.\"},{\"file\":\"rtl/ex_stage.sv\",\"description\":\"Emit conditional-branch training information and recover only when actual direction differs from the carried prediction, preserving target-misalignment traps and RVFI pc_wdata.\"},{\"file\":\"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\",\"description\":\"Treat predictor recovery as the existing redirect and flush event without changing load-use, divide, or bus-backpressure interlocks.\"},{\"file\":\"rtl/zcore.sv\",\"description\":\"Connect registered predictor lookup, EX training, and recovery while preserving the nret=1 top-level and retirement interface.\"},{\"file\":\"test/test_pipeline.py\",\"description\":\"Test predictor warm-up and taken hits, loop exit recovery, lookup cancellation across imem stalls and redirects, alias misses, misaligned targets, and ordered RVFI retirement.\"}]},\"implementation_notes\":\"\"}\n{\"id\":\"hyp-20260711-003-r9s2\",\"outcome\":\"regression\",\"fitness\":132.79,\"hypothesis\":{\"motivation\":\"The dynamic one-entry loop predictors consistently reduced the CoreMark cycle\\ncount by about four percent, proving that a repeated conditional backedge is\\nworth predicting, but their state, training network, tag validation, and wide\\nfetch muxes cost far more Fmax than the saved cycles. Because the fitness\\nworkload and linked CoreMark image are fixed, the hottest profitable backedge\\ncan instead be represented as synthesis constants with no predictor array or\\nupdate path.\\n\",\"hypothesis\":\"Profile the fixed CoreMark image and specialize for exactly one hottest\\nsame-region conditional backedge. Recognize it in fetch with the minimum\\ncollision-safe low-PC signature, replace only the target's low address slice\\nwith a constant, and carry a single predicted-taken bit to EX. Keep the PC's\\nupper bits as direct wires and add no counter, tag register, target register,\\ntraining feedback, or general control-instruction decode. EX must validate\\nboth branch direction and the reconstructed predicted target, redirecting to\\nthe architectural next PC for loop exit, signature alias, different program\\ncontents, stalls, traps, or target mismatch. This highly selective predictor\\nshould retain a meaningful fraction of the measured loop-cycle benefit while\\nmaking the fetch addition small enough to preserve the current 198 MHz Fmax.\\n\",\"expected_impact\":{\"fitness_delta_pct\":2,\"confidence\":\"low\"},\"changes\":[{\"file\":\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\",\"description\":\"Add only a one-bit predicted-taken marker to IF/ID and ID/EX; do not add a predicted-PC or target payload.\"},{\"file\":\"rtl/if_stage.sv\",\"description\":\"Implement one synthesis-constant, profile-selected hot-backedge signature and a narrow constant-low-target selection with upper PC bits bypassed, preserving redirect and imem-stall priority.\"},{\"file\":\"rtl/id_stage.sv\",\"description\":\"Latch the one-bit prediction marker with the instruction and clear it through all existing flush and bubble paths.\"},{\"file\":\"rtl/ex_stage.sv\",\"description\":\"Reconstruct and validate the fixed prediction, suppress redirect only on an exact correct prediction, and recover to the resolved architectural next PC on direction, target, alias, or trap mismatch.\"},{\"file\":\"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\",\"description\":\"Treat only EX prediction recovery as the existing redirect and flush event without changing load-use, divide, or bus-backpressure interlocks.\"},{\"file\":\"rtl/zcore.sv\",\"description\":\"Connect the prediction marker and recovery path while preserving the nret=1 top-level ports and RVFI retirement contract.\"},{\"file\":\"test/test_pipeline.py\",\"description\":\"Add tests for hot-loop warm execution and exit, low-PC aliases containing branches and non-branches, target mismatch, misalignment, and randomized imem/dmem stalls with ordered RVFI retirement.\"}]},\"implementation_notes\":\"\"}\n{\"id\":\"hyp-20260711-003-r10s2\",\"outcome\":\"regression\",\"fitness\":403.44,\"hypothesis\":{\"motivation\":\"Prior one-entry loop predictors cut CoreMark cycles by about four percent, so\\npredicting a frequent taken backedge has demonstrated IPC value. Their fitness\\nlosses came from tag/decode logic and a separate full-width target mux on the\\nfetch-to-PC path. The fixed-image attempt also failed to prioritize the exact\\nEX recovery target, so it did not establish that a minimal fixed predictor is\\nunprofitable when recovery and the PC datapath are structured correctly.\\n\",\"hypothesis\":\"Profile exactly one hottest conditional backedge, but recognize it one cycle\\nearly from the preceding sequential PC and register only a prediction-valid\\nbit. When that branch PC is reached, reuse the existing PC increment adder by\\nselecting its B-immediate constant as the addend instead of 4; do not add a\\ntarget adder, target register, predictor table, training path, or full-width\\ntarget-result mux. Cancel the lookahead on any stall, redirect, or nonsequential\\ntransition, and carry one predicted marker with the instruction. EX compares\\nthe resolved architectural next PC with the implied fixed target and gives\\nrecovery unconditional priority, selecting redirect_target for loop exit,\\nsignature mismatch, arbitrary instruction contents, traps, and aliases. This\\nshould retain the measured hot-loop cycle benefit while limiting the live PC\\npath addition to a registered bit and a narrow constant-addend selection.\\n\",\"expected_impact\":{\"fitness_delta_pct\":3,\"confidence\":\"low\"},\"changes\":[{\"file\":\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\",\"description\":\"Add a single predicted-hot-backedge marker to IF/ID and ID/EX without carrying a target or predicted PC.\"},{\"file\":\"rtl/if_stage.sv\",\"description\":\"Implement exact preceding-PC lookahead, cancellation on stalls or discontinuities, and an addend-select PC update that reuses the existing incrementer; EX redirect_target must have highest priority.\"},{\"file\":\"rtl/id_stage.sv\",\"description\":\"Latch the one-bit prediction marker with the instruction and clear it on every existing flush or bubble path.\"},{\"file\":\"rtl/ex_stage.sv\",\"description\":\"Validate the fixed prediction against the resolved architectural next PC and issue exact recovery for not-taken exits, aliases, non-control instructions, target mismatch, or traps.\"},{\"file\":\"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\",\"description\":\"Route only prediction recovery through the existing redirect flush behavior while preserving all load, divide, and bus-backpressure interlocks.\"},{\"file\":\"rtl/zcore.sv\",\"description\":\"Connect the prediction marker and exact EX recovery path without changing the nret=1 top-level or RVFI retirement contract.\"},{\"file\":\"test/test_pipeline.py\",\"description\":\"Test correct hot-backedge prediction, loop exit, fixed-PC non-branch aliases, redirect priority, and randomized imem/dmem stalls with ordered RVFI retirement.\"}]},\"implementation_notes\":\"\"}\n{\"id\":\"hyp-20260711-003-r11s2\",\"outcome\":\"regression\",\"fitness\":283.83,\"hypothesis\":{\"motivation\":\"Prior predictors saved several percent of CoreMark cycles but lost much more\\nFmax by placing lookup, target generation, or prediction selection on the\\nordinary fetch-to-PC path. Repeated taken loop branches still pay a redirect\\nbubble even though their target and the first target instruction have already\\nbeen observed, leaving an opportunity that need not speculate ahead of EX.\\n\",\"hypothesis\":\"Add a one-entry branch-folding buffer that learns the first instruction at a\\ntaken direct branch or JAL target. On a later resolved-taken control transfer,\\nrequire an exact cached source-PC and control-instruction match; on a hit,\\ninject the cached target instruction directly into ID/EX and redirect fetch to\\nthe cached target-plus-four address. On a miss, use the existing redirect and\\ncapture the target instruction when imem actually accepts it. This removes one\\nbubble from repeated taken backedges without predicting the branch direction,\\nadding a lookup to the sequential fetch-PC cone, or carrying prediction\\nmetadata through the pipeline. Restrict folding to direct controls, invalidate\\non replacement/reset, and qualify replay with all bus and pipeline holds so\\narbitrary programs, loop exits, traps, and exactly-once RVFI retirement retain\\nthe existing behavior.\\n\",\"expected_impact\":{\"fitness_delta_pct\":2,\"confidence\":\"low\"},\"changes\":[{\"file\":\"rtl/branch_fold_buffer.sv\",\"description\":\"Add a one-entry direct-control tag, cached target instruction, target-plus-four value, miss-fill handshake, and exact resolved-taken hit qualification.\"},{\"file\":\"rtl/ex_stage.sv\",\"description\":\"Expose the resolved direct-control identity and taken target needed for exact fold lookup while preserving architectural redirect, misalignment-trap, divider, and RVFI behavior.\"},{\"file\":\"rtl/id_stage.sv\",\"description\":\"Accept a fold-hit instruction payload with priority over redirect bubbling only when the pipeline can advance, recording the cached target PC and normal decoded/RVFI fields.\"},{\"file\":\"rtl/if_stage.sv\",\"description\":\"On a qualified fold hit, use the cached target-plus-four as the existing resolved redirect destination; keep ordinary sequential fetch and redirect-miss paths unchanged.\"},{\"file\":\"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\",\"description\":\"Suppress the normal ID/EX redirect bubble only for an accepted fold injection, while retaining redirect priority and every load, divide, imem, and dmem backpressure rule.\"},{\"file\":\"rtl/zcore.sv\",\"description\":\"Wire fold lookup, target fill, replay injection, and hit-qualified redirect selection without changing the nret=1 top-level or RVFI interface.\"},{\"file\":\"test/test_pipeline.py\",\"description\":\"Test cold miss and fill, repeated taken loop hits, not-taken exits, changed control instructions, replacement, reset, and randomized imem/dmem stalls with exact ordered RVFI retirement.\"}]},\"implementation_notes\":\"\"}\n{\n  \"$schema\": \"http://json-schema.org/draft-07/schema#\",\n  \"type\": \"object\",\n  \"required\": [\"id\",\"title\",\"category\",\"motivation\",\"hypothesis\",\"expected_impact\",\"changes\"],\n  \"properties\": {\n    \"id\":       {\"type\": \"string\", \"pattern\": \"^hyp-[0-9]{8}-[0-9]{3}(-r[0-9]+s[0-9]+)?$\"},\n    \"title\":    {\"type\": \"string\", \"minLength\": 5},\n    \"category\": {\"type\": \"string\", \"enum\": [\"micro_opt\",\"structural\",\"extension\",\"predictor\",\"memory\"]},\n    \"motivation\":  {\"type\": \"string\", \"minLength\": 20},\n    \"hypothesis\":  {\"type\": \"string\", \"minLength\": 20},\n    \"expected_impact\": {\n      \"type\": \"object\",\n      \"required\": [\"fitness_delta_pct\",\"confidence\"],\n      \"properties\": {\n        \"fitness_delta_pct\": {\"type\": \"integer\"},\n        \"confidence\": {\"type\": \"string\", \"enum\": [\"low\",\"medium\",\"high\"]}\n      }\n    },\n    \"changes\": {\n      \"type\": \"array\", \"minItems\": 1,\n      \"items\": {\n        \"type\": \"object\",\n        \"required\": [\"file\",\"description\"],\n        \"properties\": {\n          \"file\":        {\n            \"type\": \"string\",\n            \"description\": \"Must be under rtl/ (any extension) or be a test/test_*.py cocotb suite. Anything else is rejected by the runtime sandbox check.\",\n            \"pattern\": \"^(rtl/[^.].*|test/test_[^/]+\\\\.py)$\"\n          },\n          \"description\": {\"type\": \"string\"}\n        }\n      }\n    }\n  }\n}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"agent_message","text":"The history shows every stateful or target-caching predictor lost primarily on the fetch PC timing cone. I’m comparing the least harmful attempt against its measured cycle/Fmax split to choose an angle that changes that tradeoff rather than adding another BTB variant."}}
