{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,220p' cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260711-003-r2s2.yaml && git diff --check && rg -n 'hypothesis.schema|jsonschema' tools Makefile -g '*.py' -g 'Makefile' | head -30\"","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20260711-003-r2s2\ntitle: \"Predict direct JAL targets in fetch\"\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  The pipeline currently treats every JAL as an EX-stage redirect, discarding the\n  already fetched target instruction and paying a control bubble for direct calls\n  and unconditional jumps. A JAL target is encoded entirely in the fetched\n  instruction, so it can be predicted exactly without a BTB, history table, or\n  aliasing risk. The previous combined JAL and conditional-branch proposal failed\n  during implementation compilation, making a narrower predictor the best path to\n  retain useful control-flow speedup with much lower RTL and timing risk.\nhypothesis: |\n  Decode only the JAL opcode and J-immediate in IF, select pc+imm as the predicted\n  next fetch address, and carry that predicted next PC to EX. EX will compare the\n  architecturally resolved next PC against the prediction and redirect/flush only\n  on a mismatch, including recovery to pc+4 for a misaligned JAL trap. All other\n  instructions remain predict-not-taken and preserve the existing recovery path.\n  This stateless, always-correct prediction should remove the EX redirect penalty\n  for every aligned direct call and jump while adding only a small immediate adder\n  and metadata register, so iterations per cycle should rise with little Fmax loss.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 4\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n    description: \"Add predicted-next-PC metadata to the IF/ID and ID/EX payloads.\"\n  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n    description: \"Recognize fetched JAL instructions, form their J-immediate target, advance the PC to that target, and emit the selected predicted next PC while respecting stalls and recovery priority.\"\n  - file: rtl/id_stage.sv\n    description: \"Latch predicted-next-PC metadata with the instruction into ID/EX.\"\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: \"Generate a recovery redirect only when resolved next PC differs from the carried prediction, including misaligned-JAL trap recovery, while keeping architectural RVFI pc_wdata unchanged.\"\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: \"Wire the EX recovery indication and target through the existing IF and hazard redirect paths without changing the nret=1 interface.\"\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: \"Add directed checks that aligned JAL reaches its target without a recovery bubble and that misaligned JAL traps, suppresses rd writeback, and recovers to pc+4 under bus stalls.\"\ntools/orchestrator.py:6:import jsonschema, yaml\ntools/orchestrator.py:48:HYP_SCHEMA     = json.loads(Path(\"schemas/hypothesis.schema.json\").read_text())\ntools/orchestrator.py:447:    jsonschema.validate(hyp, HYP_SCHEMA)\ntools/tournament.py:54:# The hypothesis schema's `category` enum, in the order the brief specifies.\ntools/tournament.py:218:    import jsonschema\ntools/tournament.py:262:    except (jsonschema.ValidationError, FileNotFoundError, yaml.YAMLError) as e:\ntools/agents/static_agent.py:94:        \"      No-op placeholder to satisfy the hypothesis schema's\\n\"\ntools/agents/hypothesis.py:282:## Required YAML structure (validates against schemas/hypothesis.schema.json)\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
